const e=[{id:1,code:"FACT-101",icon:"HardHat",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Personal protective equipment (PPE)",subtitle:"Personal protective equipment",summary:"Knowing your personal protective equipment and how to use it correctly",blocks:[{body:"Personal protective equipment (PPE) is the last line of defense against injury: before relying on it, hazards must be eliminated or reduced through engineering controls and safe work procedures (Hierarchy of Controls). In factories, wearing PPE is not optional; it is mandatory."},{heading:"1. Main types of PPE",body:"Each part of the body has its own specific equipment:",points:["Head: hard hat — protects against falling objects and impacts.","Eyes: safety glasses — protect against dust, chemicals and flying particles.","Ears: hearing protection (earmuffs or earplugs) — against loud machine noise (85 dB(A) and above).","Hands: gloves — against cuts, heat and chemicals.","Feet: steel-toe safety boots — against crushing and heavy falling objects.","Body: high-visibility (hi-vis) clothing in bright colors — so drivers and other workers can see you."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/ppe_tutorial.mp4",title:"PPE",description:"The correct way to put it on"}],quiz:[{question:"According to European standards, when does wearing hearing protection become mandatory?",options:[{text:"50 dB",correct:!1},{text:"70 dB",correct:!1},{text:"85 dB(A) and above (averaged over an 8-hour workday)",correct:!0},{text:"120 dB",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under European Directive 2003/10/EC, when the average noise level over an 8-hour workday reaches 85 dB(A), hearing protection must be worn. OSHA also requires a hearing conservation program at 85 dBA."},{question:"PPE is the last line of defense against injury. What must come first?",options:[{text:"Buying more expensive protective equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Eliminating or reducing the hazard",correct:!0},{text:"Asking workers to work faster to cut exposure time",correct:!1},{text:"Waiting until an injury shows where the hazard is",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before relying on PPE, hazards must be eliminated or reduced through engineering controls and safe work procedures (Hierarchy of Controls); PPE is the last line of defense and is still mandatory in factories."},{question:"Why do workers wear high-visibility (hi-vis) clothing in bright colors?",options:[{text:"To protect the skin from chemicals and heat",correct:!1},{text:"To show which department each worker is in",correct:!1},{text:"To keep the body warm in cold workshops",correct:!1},{text:"So drivers and other workers can see them",correct:!0}],explanation:"Hi-vis clothing in bright colors makes a worker easy to see for drivers and other workers; protection from chemicals and heat comes from other PPE, such as gloves."}]},{id:2,code:"FACT-102",icon:"Cog",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Machine safety",subtitle:"Machine safety and guarding",summary:"Protecting workers from the moving parts of machinery",blocks:[{body:"Industrial machines are extremely powerful and can cause serious injuries or the loss of a limb in a split second."},{heading:"1. Machine guards",body:"These safeguards must be fitted on all hazardous machines:",points:["Fixed guards: to enclose moving belts and gears.","Emergency stop button (E-Stop): must be within the operator's reach and colored red on a yellow background.","Safety stop sensors: the machine stops if a hand or body comes close to the danger zone."]},{heading:"2. Golden rules",body:"Before and during work:",points:["Never remove a machine guard while the machine is running.","Do not wear loose clothing, chains or rings, and tie back long hair, so nothing gets caught in the machine.","If a machine malfunctions, stop it immediately with the E-Stop button, and never put your hand inside the machine without applying the LOTO procedure."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/machine_guard.png",title:"Machine guards",description:"An example of a correct guard"}],quiz:[{question:"Is it acceptable to remove a machine guard while the machine is running?",options:[{text:"Yes, if necessary",correct:!1},{text:"No, it is never acceptable",correct:!0},{text:"Only for cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"All of the answers are correct",correct:!1}],explanation:"Removing a machine guard while the machine is running is one of the most dangerous behaviors. Cleaning and repairs are carried out only after the machine has been stopped and locked out (LOTO)."},{question:"A machine malfunctions while you are working on it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop it at once with the E-Stop button",correct:!0},{text:"Reach in quickly and clear the jam by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Keep it running and tell the supervisor later",correct:!1},{text:"Remove the guard to see what went wrong",correct:!1}],explanation:"If a machine malfunctions, stop it immediately with the E-Stop button, and never put your hand inside the machine without applying the LOTO procedure."},{question:"Why must you not wear loose clothing, chains or rings near machines?",options:[{text:"They make the controls harder to read",correct:!1},{text:"They break the dress code, nothing more",correct:!1},{text:"They can get caught in the machine",correct:!0},{text:"They can damage the machine guards",correct:!1}],explanation:"Loose clothing, chains, rings and long hair can get caught in the machine, and a machine can cause a serious injury in a split second; tie back long hair too."}]},{id:3,code:"FACT-103",icon:"PlugZap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electrical safety",subtitle:"Industrial electrical safety (LOTO)",summary:"Electrical hazards and the lockout/tagout (LOTO) system",blocks:[{body:"Electricity in factories runs at high voltage; an electric shock can cause death or severe burns."},{heading:"1. Lockout/tagout system (LOTO)",body:"This is the most important procedure before repairing or cleaning machinery:",points:["Lockout: isolate the main power source and apply your personal lock to it.",'Tagout: attach a tag that reads "Work in progress — do not switch on", with your name and the date.',"The key stays only with the person doing the repair; before starting, verify that the machine is truly de-energized."]},{heading:"2. Cable inspection",body:"Always check:",points:["Do not use cut cables or cables with exposed wires.","Electricity and water do not mix; do not use electrical equipment in wet areas."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/loto_safety.mp4",title:"LOTO",description:"How do we use the lock?"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the purpose of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"Isolating and locking out energy before repairs",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing machine speed",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"The LOTO system is used so that no one can switch the power back on while a worker is working on the machine (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147)."},{question:"You have applied your lock and tag. What must you do before starting the repair?",options:[{text:"Give the key to a coworker for safekeeping",correct:!1},{text:"Switch the machine on briefly to test the lock",correct:!1},{text:"Verify that the machine is truly de-energized",correct:!0},{text:"Start at once, since the lock alone makes it safe",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before starting, verify that the machine is truly de-energized; the key stays only with the person doing the repair, so no one can switch the power back on."},{question:"You find a cable that is cut and has exposed wires. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use it only in dry areas",correct:!1},{text:"Do not use it",correct:!0},{text:"Wrap it in tape and keep using it",correct:!1},{text:"Use it for light tools only",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not use cut cables or cables with exposed wires; an electric shock can cause death or severe burns."}]},{id:4,code:"FIRE-104",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers — complete training",subtitle:"Fire extinguisher — complete training (PASS)",summary:"A detailed look at extinguisher types, fire classes and the PASS technique",blocks:[{body:"A fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stage. In a factory, every employee must know how to use an extinguisher quickly and correctly. Knowing the types of extinguishers and the classes of fire will protect your life and the lives of your coworkers. A portable extinguisher discharges for only a few seconds, so it must be aimed correctly. If the fire grows or the smoke becomes heavy, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115."},{heading:"1. Fire classes under NFPA",body:"Under the American system (NFPA), fires are divided into 5 classes, and each class requires a suitable extinguisher. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.",points:["Class A (ordinary solids): wood, paper, fabric, plastic. Extinguisher: water, foam, ABC powder.","Class B (flammable liquids and gases): gasoline, kerosene, oil, alcohol, gas. Liquids: CO₂, ABC powder or foam; for alcohol and other polar solvents, CO₂, dry chemical or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop. (Never use water!)","Class C (energized electrical equipment): switches, transformers, electrical panels, generators. Extinguisher: CO₂, ABC powder, clean agent. (Do not use water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking, as on some water-mist units!)","Class D (metals): combustible metals — magnesium, titanium, sodium and aluminum powder. Extinguisher: only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. (Water can cause an explosion!)","Class K (kitchen): cooking oils and fats. Extinguisher: wet chemical. (Water spreads burning oil!) — under EN 2 this is Class F."]},{heading:"2. Types of extinguishers in factories",body:"Color is not the fire class. Under BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols. Classes below are in NFPA letters:",points:["Water extinguisher: all red, no band — for Class A only (wood, paper). Never use it on electrical equipment or oil!","CO₂ extinguisher: black band in the UK — for Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). It releases a very cold gas and the horn becomes extremely cold; hold only the handle, never the horn, or you will get a cold burn! CO₂ has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires: start from the distance on its label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. In small rooms, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ reduces the oxygen level.","ABC dry powder extinguisher: blue band in the UK — for Classes A, B and C (not D or K). It is the most common type. It discharges a white powder; drawbacks: it reduces visibility and contaminates equipment.","Foam extinguisher (AFFF): cream band in the UK — for Classes A and B. It spreads a foam blanket over the liquid and cuts off the oxygen. Do not use it on electrical equipment!","Clean agent extinguisher: its rating depends on the model, so read the label (often B and C in NFPA letters; some models also A) — for sensitive equipment and server rooms. It leaves no residue and does not damage electronic equipment.","Special extinguisher (Class D): for combustible metals in factories — its agent must be listed on the label for the metal in use. Never put water on burning metal!"]},{heading:"3. The PASS technique — in detail",body:"Before you use an extinguisher, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers; Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions:",points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin prevents the extinguisher from being discharged by accident.","A = Aim: aim the extinguisher nozzle at the base of the fire — not at the flames! The fuel of the fire is at the base.","S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handles firmly to discharge the agent.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side across the base of the fire until the fire is completely out; then keep watching the area so the fire does not reignite."]},{heading:"4. Monthly extinguisher inspection",body:"Visual check every month, by the person in charge (NFPA 10):",points:["Pressure gauge, if there is one: the needle must be in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Safety pin and tamper seal: must be in place and unbroken.","Nozzle: must not be broken or blocked.","Extinguisher body: must not be rusted, cracked or damaged.","Inspection tag and label: the tag shows the date of the last inspection; the label is readable and faces out; the extinguisher is in its place, visible and easy to reach.","Note: keep these separate. Inspection and maintenance once a year by a qualified technician; recharge after any use, even a short one; 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years; hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder) every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers, and every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. A test date is not a replacement date. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"5. Specific hazards in factories",body:"A factory is a high-risk environment for these reasons:",points:["Chemicals: solvents, alcohol, thinner — all of them are Class B.","High-voltage electricity: electrical panels and transformers — Class C (NFPA).","Wood dust and flour dust: in some factories these can cause a dust explosion.","Welding and cutting: welding sparks can start Class A and Class B fires.","Fuel storage: must be in a dedicated room or outside the building, with a suitable extinguisher (foam or powder) nearby.","Placement: mount extinguishers near doors and walkways; the travel distance to a Class A extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters (75 feet), and for liquids 9-15 meters (NFPA 10)."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fire_ext_factory.mp4",title:"Fire extinguishers in factories",description:"The PASS technique and extinguisher types on video"}],quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ is suitable for flammable liquids (B) and electrical equipment (C), because it leaves no residue and does not conduct electricity. Under EN 2, C means gas fires, and CO₂ is not rated for them."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where should the extinguisher nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the flames",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Away from the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, because that is where the fuel is."},{question:"Why must water never be poured on a cooking-oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"It has no effect",correct:!1},{text:"Water spreads the burning oil and creates a large flare-up",correct:!0},{text:"The oil absorbs the water",correct:!1},{text:"The water itself burns",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water on hot oil boils instantly and throws the burning oil into the air, creating a large fireball. Use a wet chemical extinguisher or a lid, and turn off the heat."}]},{id:5,code:"AID-105",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"First aid and CPR — complete training",summary:"Complete training in first aid, CPR and emergency response in factories",blocks:[{body:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before medical teams arrive. In factories the risk of injury is very high — from cuts and burns to electric shock and falls from height. Every employee must know how to call the ambulance (122), perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect the casualty until the ambulance arrives. This lesson follows the AHA 2025 guidelines and Stop the Bleed."},{heading:"1. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) — in detail",body:"CPR is a life-saving procedure used when a person's heart stops and normal breathing is absent. Follow these steps:",points:["1. Scene safety: before anything else, make sure the area is safe (no live electricity, fire or gas), so you do not become a second casualty.",'2. Responsiveness: tap the casualty firmly on the shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you okay? Can you hear me?"',"3. Call: if there is no response and the person is not breathing or is only gasping, call 122 immediately (or have someone else call) and ask for an AED to be brought.","4. Chest compressions: place the heel of one hand on the center of the chest (the lower half of the breastbone) and the other hand on top. Give 30 hard, fast compressions: depth 5-6 cm, rate 100-120 compressions per minute and let the chest recoil fully.","5. Rescue breaths: tilt the head back and lift the chin, pinch the nose and give 2 breaths (each about 1 second, until the chest rises). If you cannot or are unwilling to give breaths, continue chest compressions only.","6. Continue (30:2): keep repeating 30 compressions and 2 breaths until the ambulance arrives, an AED is ready or the casualty starts breathing normally.","7. Using the AED: as soon as it arrives, switch it on, attach the pads to the bare chest and follow the device's voice prompts; do not touch the casualty while a shock is delivered."]},{heading:"2. Bleeding control (Stop the Bleed)",body:"Severe bleeding can kill a person within a short time, so call 122 and take these steps quickly:",points:["Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press hard with both hands. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another one on top and keep pressing.","Wound packing: if the wound is deep (for example in the thigh, armpit or neck), pack clean cloth or hemostatic gauze into the wound and press hard. Do not elevate the injured limb and do not look for pressure points.","Tourniquet: if bleeding from an arm or leg is life-threatening, apply a tourniquet early, 5-7 cm above the wound (toward the body), tighten it firmly, write down the time and never loosen it.","Embedded object: if a piece of metal or glass is embedded in the wound, never pull it out! That would increase the bleeding. Apply pressure around it and stabilize it."]},{heading:"3. Treating burns",body:"Burns in factories are dangerous; treat them as follows, and call 122 for large burns or burns to the face or hands:",points:["Thermal burns: immediately cool the burn under cool running water (not ice water) for 20 minutes. Quickly remove rings and watches before the area swells.","Chemical burns: remove contaminated clothing and flush the skin with plenty of running water for 20 minutes or longer, and check the substance's SDS.","Electrical burns: first, cut off the power! Then cool the burn as you would a thermal burn and call 122, because electricity can cause damage to the heart and inside the body.","Caution: do not burst burn blisters, and do not use ice, butter or grease, toothpaste or ointments without medical advice."]},{heading:"4. Fractures and spinal injuries",body:"After a fall from height or when a fracture is suspected:",points:["Splinting: if a fracture is suspected, do not move the injured limb. Immobilize it in the position found with a piece of wood or cardboard to reduce pain.","Protecting the back and neck: if someone has fallen from height, do not move them unless the location is dangerous (for example, fire), because moving them can cause paralysis. Keep the head still and call 122.","Cooling: to reduce swelling, wrap ice in a piece of cloth and place it on the painful area for 20 minutes; do not put ice directly on the skin."]},{heading:"5. Electric shock and rescue",body:"Electricity can stop the heart; follow these procedures:",points:["Cut off the power immediately: do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off, because the current will pass through you too.","Safe separation: if you cannot cut off the power and the voltage is low, use a dry piece of wood or plastic to move the wire away from the casualty while standing on something dry. Do not go near high-voltage lines; call 115.","Medical check: everyone who has received an electric shock, even if they feel fine, must go to the hospital for a heart check; call 122."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/cpr_factory.mp4",title:"CPR and first aid",description:"CPR training and helping injured workers in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"In CPR, what should the chest compression rate be per minute?",options:[{text:"50-70",correct:!1},{text:"80-90",correct:!1},{text:"100-120",correct:!0},{text:"150+",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to AHA 2025, the compression rate is 100-120 compressions per minute, at a depth of 5-6 cm for adults, so that blood reaches the brain."},{question:"If a sharp object is embedded in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I keep my hands away from it and do nothing",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pulling the object out will increase the bleeding. It must stay in place and be stabilized until a doctor removes it at the hospital."},{question:"If someone has received an electric shock, what is the first thing you do?",options:[{text:"I grab their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the power",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"The power source must be cut off first so the current does not pass through you too; then call 122 and start CPR if needed."}]},{id:6,code:"CHEM-106",icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Chemicals (SDS)",subtitle:"Chemical safety and labeling",summary:"Handling chemicals and recognizing their hazards",blocks:[{body:"Chemicals in factories can cause skin burns, poisoning, fires or explosions."},{heading:"1. What is an SDS?",body:"The Safety Data Sheet (SDS) gives the information for each substance in 16 sections, including:",points:["The composition of the substance and its hazards.","Which PPE is required (respirator, gloves, eye protection).","First aid if the substance contacts the body, and firefighting measures."]},{heading:"2. GHS pictograms",body:"Some of the symbols found on containers and packaging:",points:["Flame: flammable substance.","Skull and crossbones: acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance.","Corrosion: an acidic or alkaline substance that eats away skin and metals."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/ghs_pictograms.png",title:"SDS",description:"GHS hazard pictograms"}],quiz:[{question:"Where can we find out which respirator is required for a chemical?",options:[{text:"On the signs on the wall",correct:!1},{text:"In the SDS",correct:!0},{text:"Ask a coworker",correct:!1},{text:"No respirator is needed",correct:!1}],explanation:"The SDS provides all the protection information specific to that substance, including the type of PPE required (Section 8)."},{question:"What does the skull and crossbones pictogram on a container mean?",options:[{text:"A flammable substance that catches fire easily",correct:!1},{text:"An acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance",correct:!0},{text:"An acid or alkali that eats away skin and metals",correct:!1},{text:"A substance that harms only the environment",correct:!1}],explanation:"The skull and crossbones marks an acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance; the flame marks a flammable substance and the corrosion pictogram an acid or alkali."},{question:"Besides the hazards and the required PPE, what else does the SDS tell you?",options:[{text:"The price of the substance and its supplier",correct:!1},{text:"The cleaning schedule for the chemical store",correct:!1},{text:"The names of workers allowed to use it",correct:!1},{text:"First aid and firefighting measures",correct:!0}],explanation:"The SDS gives the information for each substance in 16 sections, including its hazards, the PPE required, first aid if the substance contacts the body and firefighting measures."}]},{id:7,code:"HGHT-107",icon:"Construction",accent:"#0284c7",title:"Working at height",subtitle:"Fall protection and ladders",summary:"Preventing falls from scaffolding, roofs and ladders",blocks:[{body:"Falls from height are one of the leading causes of death in factories and on construction sites."},{heading:"1. Fall protection",body:"Any work at height (OSHA: from 1.2 meters in general industry and from 1.8 meters in construction) requires the following:",points:["Full-body harness: worn correctly and fastened securely.","Lanyard: must be attached to a secure anchor point.","Guardrails: around scaffolding, roofs and open edges."]},{heading:"2. Ladder safety",body:"The 3-point contact rule:",points:["You must always have 3 limbs (two feet and one hand, or two hands and one foot) in contact with the ladder.","The ladder angle must be 4:1 (for every 4 meters of height, the base of the ladder is 1 meter away from the wall)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fall_protection.mp4",title:"Working at height",description:"Using a harness"}],quiz:[{question:"What does the 3-point rule on a ladder mean?",options:[{text:"3 people on it at the same time",correct:!1},{text:"Stay on it for 3 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Always keep 3 limbs in contact with the ladder",correct:!0},{text:"Tie the ladder with 3 ropes",correct:!1}],explanation:"The 3-point rule ensures that the worker keeps their balance and does not fall."},{question:"A ladder reaches 4 meters up a wall. How far from the wall should its base be?",options:[{text:"Half a meter",correct:!1},{text:"1 meter",correct:!0},{text:"2 meters",correct:!1},{text:"4 meters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ladder angle must be 4:1: for every 4 meters of height, the base of the ladder is 1 meter away from the wall."},{question:"Where must the lanyard of a full-body harness be attached?",options:[{text:"To the worker's own tool belt",correct:!1},{text:"To the ladder the worker is standing on",correct:!1},{text:"To a secure anchor point",correct:!0},{text:"To any nearby pipe or cable",correct:!1}],explanation:"The lanyard must be attached to a secure anchor point, and the full-body harness must be worn correctly and fastened securely."}]},{id:8,code:"CONF-108",icon:"Container",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Confined spaces",subtitle:"Confined space safety",summary:"Working inside tanks, pipes and manholes",blocks:[{body:"Confined spaces are dangerous because the air inside is not refreshed, oxygen levels can drop and deadly gases can build up."},{heading:"1. Main hazards",body:"Before you enter, be aware of:",points:["Oxygen deficiency (below 19.5%): causes asphyxiation.","Toxic gases such as CO (odorless) and H₂S (which knocks out the sense of smell at high concentrations).","Risk of fire and explosion if flammable gases or vapors are present."]},{heading:"2. Entry procedures",body:"These conditions must be in place:",points:["Air testing with a gas monitor before entry and during the work.","An attendant who stays outside, keeps watch on the entrant inside and never enters under any circumstances.","An entry permit, means of communication and a rescue plan must be in place."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/confined_entry.png",title:"Confined spaces",description:"Air testing"}],quiz:[{question:"Is it acceptable for the attendant outside to enter without equipment to rescue a coworker?",options:[{text:"Yes, out of humanity",correct:!1},{text:"No, because they will be overcome too",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if they hold their breath",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"According to NIOSH studies, a large share of confined-space deaths are people who entered without equipment or training to attempt a rescue. The attendant must call the rescue team and 115."},{question:"When must the air in a confined space be tested with a gas monitor?",options:[{text:"Only once, on the first day of the job",correct:!1},{text:"Only if someone notices an unusual smell",correct:!1},{text:"After the work is finished",correct:!1},{text:"Before entry and during the work",correct:!0}],explanation:"The air is tested before entry and during the work, because oxygen levels can drop and deadly gases can build up; CO is odorless, so smelling the air is not a test."},{question:"Inside a tank, a worker suddenly can no longer smell H₂S. What could this mean?",options:[{text:"The gas has cleared and the air is safe to breathe",correct:!1},{text:"The gas may have knocked out the sense of smell",correct:!0},{text:"The ventilation fan has started to work properly",correct:!1},{text:"The gas has turned into harmless water vapor",correct:!1}],explanation:"At high concentrations H₂S knocks out the sense of smell, so losing the smell does not mean the gas is gone; only a gas monitor shows whether the air is safe."}]},{id:9,code:"NOIS-109",icon:"Ear",accent:"#db2777",title:"Noise and hearing protection",subtitle:"Hearing conservation",summary:"Protection against the noise of heavy machinery",blocks:[{body:"Deafness or hearing loss caused by factory noise develops gradually and cannot be cured."},{heading:"1. Noise levels",body:"When does noise become a hazard?",points:["85 dB(A): the threshold at which the hazard begins, for an 8-hour workday.","100 dB(A): safe without protection for only 15 minutes a day (NIOSH).","Warning sign: if you notice ringing in your ears (tinnitus) after work, the noise was too loud."]},{heading:"2. Protective equipment",body:"Hearing protection devices (HPD):",points:["Earplugs: give good protection when inserted correctly.","Earmuffs: easy to put on and suitable for loud noise.","Wear both together in very noisy areas."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/hearing_protection.mp4",title:"Noise",description:"How do you use hearing protection?"}],quiz:[{question:"If the noise level reaches 100 dB(A), how long can you stay without hearing protection according to NIOSH?",options:[{text:"1 hour",correct:!1},{text:"15 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"4 hours",correct:!1},{text:"All day",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIOSH, every 3 dB increase halves the safe exposure time: 85 dB(A) for 8 hours, and 100 dB(A) for only 15 minutes."},{question:"After a shift, you notice ringing in your ears (tinnitus). What does this tell you?",options:[{text:"Your ears are getting used to the noise",correct:!1},{text:"The noise was too loud",correct:!0},{text:"Your earplugs were too tight",correct:!1},{text:"It is normal and means nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ringing in the ears after work is a warning sign that the noise was too loud; hearing loss from noise develops gradually and cannot be cured, so use hearing protection."},{question:"In very noisy areas, how should hearing protection be worn?",options:[{text:"Cotton wool instead of earplugs",correct:!1},{text:"No protection, but with short breaks",correct:!1},{text:"Earplugs and earmuffs together",correct:!0},{text:"Hands over the ears when it gets loud",correct:!1}],explanation:"In very noisy areas, wear both together: earplugs give good protection when inserted correctly, and earmuffs are easy to put on and suitable for loud noise."}]},{id:10,code:"ERGO-110",icon:"Package",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Manual handling (ergonomics)",subtitle:"Safe lifting techniques",summary:"Preventing back and muscle injuries",blocks:[{body:"Back injuries are among the most common injuries in factories, caused by lifting loads incorrectly."},{heading:"1. The correct lifting technique",body:"Following these steps protects your back:",points:["Bend your knees: use the strength of your legs, not your back.","Keep the load close to your body: the farther the load is from you, the greater the strain on your back.","Keep your back straight: do not bend your back while lifting something heavy.","Do not twist your body: if you need to move the load to one side, turn your whole body using your feet."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/safe_lifting.png",title:"Lifting a load",description:"The correct technique"}],quiz:[{question:"When lifting something heavy, where should the effort come from?",options:[{text:"The back",correct:!1},{text:"The leg muscles",correct:!0},{text:"The shoulders",correct:!1},{text:"The hands only",correct:!1}],explanation:"The leg muscles are stronger and protect the back from injury."},{question:"Where should a heavy load be while you lift it?",options:[{text:"At arm's length, away from your body",correct:!1},{text:"Above your head",correct:!1},{text:"To one side of your body",correct:!1},{text:"Close to your body",correct:!0}],explanation:"Keep the load close to your body: the farther the load is from you, the greater the strain on your back."},{question:"You need to move a load to one side. How do you turn?",options:[{text:"Twist your body and keep your feet still",correct:!1},{text:"Turn your whole body using your feet",correct:!0},{text:"Bend your back sideways toward the new spot",correct:!1},{text:"Throw the load across quickly to save effort",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not twist your body: to move the load to one side, turn your whole body using your feet and keep your back straight."}]},{id:11,code:"GAS-111",icon:"Wind",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Gases and ventilation",subtitle:"Ventilation and toxic gases",summary:"Controlling air quality and toxic gases",blocks:[{body:"Proper ventilation in a factory is important to prevent the buildup of dust and gases that cause respiratory diseases."},{heading:"1. Carbon monoxide (CO)",body:"The silent killer gas:",points:["It is colorless and odorless.","It is produced by burning fuel and by gasoline and diesel engines (such as generators and forklifts).","Symptoms: headache, dizziness, nausea and loss of consciousness. If you notice these symptoms, get outside immediately and call 122."]},{heading:"2. Types of ventilation",body:"How is the air exchanged?",points:["Natural ventilation: open doors and windows.","Mechanical ventilation: fans and local exhaust ventilation (LEV) systems.","Personal protection: filter respirators as specified in the SDS; however, filter respirators do not protect against CO or oxygen deficiency."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/ventilation_safety.mp4",title:"Gas",description:"Carbon monoxide (CO)"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is CO dangerous?",options:[{text:"Because it smells bad",correct:!1},{text:"Because it cannot be seen or smelled",correct:!0},{text:"Because it is red",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is only produced at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"Because it cannot be sensed, workers are poisoned without realizing it. Install CO detectors wherever engines are running."},{question:"While working near a running generator, you get a headache, dizziness and nausea. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take a painkiller and keep working",correct:!1},{text:"Sit down near the generator and rest",correct:!1},{text:"Put on a filter respirator and stay",correct:!1},{text:"Get outside at once and call 122",correct:!0}],explanation:"Headache, dizziness and nausea can be signs of CO poisoning; get outside immediately and call 122, because CO cannot be seen or smelled."},{question:"Does a filter respirator protect a worker against CO?",options:[{text:"No, it does not protect against CO",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, any filter respirator stops CO",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if it is worn for less than an hour",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the filter is changed every day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Filter respirators are chosen as specified in the SDS, but they do not protect against CO or oxygen deficiency; where engines run, ventilation and CO detectors are needed."}]},{id:12,code:"EVAC-112",icon:"Megaphone",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Emergency evacuation",subtitle:"Emergency assembly points",summary:"The plan for getting out during a fire or disaster",blocks:[{body:"Knowing your exit route can make the difference between life and death."},{heading:"1. Evacuation rules",body:"When the alarm sounds:",points:["Walk calmly and quickly, do not run and do not use the elevator.","Close doors behind you so fire and smoke do not spread, but do not lock them.","Go to the assembly point so that employees can be counted.","Never go back inside until the safety officer or the 115 teams give permission."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/evacuation_drill.mp4",title:"Evacuation drill",description:"Evacuation training"}],quiz:[{question:"When the alarm sounds, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Gather my belongings",correct:!1},{text:"Walk calmly toward the exit",correct:!0},{text:"Hide under a desk",correct:!1},{text:"Shout",correct:!1}],explanation:"Evacuating calmly and quickly is the best way to protect life."},{question:"What do you do with the doors as you leave during an evacuation?",options:[{text:"Lock them so no one can go back inside",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them wide open to let the smoke out",correct:!1},{text:"Close them, but do not lock them",correct:!0},{text:"Prop them open for the teams arriving later",correct:!1}],explanation:"Close doors behind you so fire and smoke do not spread, but do not lock them."},{question:"You are at the assembly point. When may you go back inside?",options:[{text:"As soon as the alarm bell stops ringing in the building",correct:!1},{text:"When the safety officer or the 115 teams allow it",correct:!0},{text:"When no more smoke comes out of the windows",correct:!1},{text:"After 10 minutes, if no fire can be seen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go back inside until the safety officer or the 115 teams give permission; the assembly point is also where employees are counted."}]},{id:13,code:"WELD-113",icon:"Sparkles",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Welding safety",subtitle:"Welding and cutting hazards",summary:"Protection from welding radiation, heat and fumes",blocks:[{body:"Welding work involves the risk of ultraviolet (UV) radiation, severe burns, and toxic gases and fumes."},{heading:"1. Eye and skin protection",body:"What welders need:",points:["A welding helmet with the correct filter shade: to protect the eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the welding arc.","Long leather gauntlets: for protection from heat and spatter.","A leather apron: to protect the body."]},{heading:"2. Fire hazard (hot work)",body:"Before starting, obtain a hot-work permit and:",points:["Clear combustible materials from around the work area (NFPA 51B: within 11 meters / 35 feet), or cover them with fire-resistant blankets.","Have a suitable fire extinguisher ready close by.","Fire watch: stays at the location during the work and for at least 1 hour after the work is finished; the area is then monitored for up to 3 more hours, as decided by the permit authorizing individual (NFPA 51B)."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/welding_safety.png",title:"Welding",description:"Wearing protective gear correctly"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does the fire watch stay at the location for at least 1 hour after welding?",options:[{text:"For cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"To prevent a hidden fire from developing",correct:!0},{text:"To cool down the metal",correct:!1},{text:"It is not necessary",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sparks often fall into cracks and materials and start a fire some time later. Under NFPA 51B (2019 edition onward), the fire watch continues for at least 1 hour after the work."},{question:"Before hot work starts, what must be done with combustible materials around the work area?",options:[{text:"Leave them in place, since a fire watch will be present",correct:!1},{text:"Remove them or cover them with fire-resistant blankets",correct:!0},{text:"Spray them with water once and start welding right away",correct:!1},{text:"Move them just behind the welder, away from direct sparks",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before starting, obtain a hot-work permit and clear combustible materials from around the work area (NFPA 51B: within 11 meters / 35 feet), or cover them with fire-resistant blankets."},{question:"What protects a welder's eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the arc?",options:[{text:"Ordinary clear safety glasses, as used for dust",correct:!1},{text:"Dark sunglasses worn under a hard hat",correct:!1},{text:"A welding helmet with the correct filter shade",correct:!0},{text:"Looking at the arc for a few seconds only",correct:!1}],explanation:"A welding helmet with the correct filter shade protects the eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the welding arc; long leather gauntlets and a leather apron protect the hands and body."}]},{id:14,code:"FORK-114",icon:"Forklift",accent:"#475569",title:"Material handling equipment",subtitle:"Forklift and vehicle safety",summary:"Forklift safety and driving inside the factory",blocks:[{body:"A forklift is a heavy machine with a risk of crushing and tip-over if it is not operated correctly."},{heading:"1. Operating rules",body:"For forklift operators:",points:["Only trained and authorized persons may operate a forklift (OSHA 1910.178).","Never carry anyone on the forks of a forklift or beside you.","Do not exceed the rated capacity of the machine, so it does not tip over, and fasten your seat belt.","Slow down and sound the horn at every corner and doorway."]},{heading:"2. Pedestrian safety",body:"If you are walking near a forklift:",points:["Make eye contact with the operator and keep a safe distance.","Walk in the marked pedestrian walkways."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/forklift_safety.mp4",title:"Forklift",description:"Recognizing blind spots"}],quiz:[{question:"If a forklift operator is carrying a tall load and cannot see ahead, what should they do?",options:[{text:"Drive fast",correct:!1},{text:"Drive in reverse",correct:!0},{text:"Sound the horn and close their eyes",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"When the load blocks the operator's view, they must drive in reverse so they can see where they are going (OSHA 1910.178)."},{question:"A coworker asks for a ride on the forks of your forklift. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Let them ride if you drive slowly",correct:!1},{text:"Let them stand on the forks and hold on",correct:!1},{text:"Let them sit beside you instead of on the forks",correct:!1},{text:"Refuse, because no one may ride on the forks",correct:!0}],explanation:"Never carry anyone on the forks of a forklift or beside you; a forklift is a heavy machine with a risk of crushing and tip-over."},{question:"You are walking near a moving forklift. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Make eye contact and keep a safe distance",correct:!0},{text:"Walk close to it so the driver sees you better",correct:!1},{text:"Cross in front of it quickly before it reaches you",correct:!1},{text:"Wave and walk on, since the driver must stop",correct:!1}],explanation:"Near a forklift, make eye contact with the operator, keep a safe distance and walk in the marked pedestrian walkways; the operator may not see you in the blind spots."}]},{id:15,code:"SIGN-115",icon:"AlertTriangle",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Safety signs",subtitle:"Safety signs and symbols",summary:"Understanding safety colors and symbols (ISO 7010)",blocks:[{body:"Safety signs warn us about hazards in a universal language."},{heading:"1. The meaning of colors (ISO 7010)",body:"Each color conveys a specific message:",points:["Red: prohibition or firefighting equipment (example: no smoking).","Yellow: warning and danger (example: electrical hazard).","Blue: mandatory (example: hard hat must be worn).","Green: safe condition and exits (example: emergency exit door)."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/safety_signs.png",title:"Safety signs",description:"Standard symbols"}],quiz:[{question:"What does a yellow sign mean?",options:[{text:"Prohibition",correct:!1},{text:"Warning and danger",correct:!0},{text:"Safe place",correct:!1},{text:"Use of water",correct:!1}],explanation:'Yellow always means "be careful" or "there is a hazard".'},{question:"A blue sign at a workshop door shows a hard hat. What does it mean?",options:[{text:"Hard hats are not allowed here",correct:!1},{text:"Warning: objects may fall here",correct:!1},{text:"A hard hat must be worn here",correct:!0},{text:"Hard hats are stored behind this door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Blue means mandatory, for example a hard hat must be worn; red means prohibition or firefighting equipment, yellow warning and green safe condition and exits."},{question:"Which color is used for safe conditions and exits, such as the emergency exit door?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1},{text:"Blue",correct:!1},{text:"Green",correct:!0}],explanation:"Green marks safe conditions and exits, such as the emergency exit door; red is for prohibition or firefighting equipment."}]},{id:16,code:"HOUS-116",icon:"ClipboardCheck",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Housekeeping",subtitle:"Order and maintenance",summary:"Organizing the workplace to reduce hazards",blocks:[{body:"A clean workplace is a safe workplace. Many accidents happen because of slipping and tripping over scattered materials."},{heading:"1. General rules",body:"For all workers:",points:["Clean up spilled oil or water as soon as possible.","Collect waste in designated bins.","Keep walkways and exits open and free of obstructions.","Return every tool to its place when the work is finished."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/clean_factory.png",title:"Housekeeping",description:"An example of a clean workplace"}],quiz:[{question:"If you see oil spilled on the floor, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it until the cleaner comes",correct:!1},{text:"Clean it up, or put a sign on it and report it",correct:!0},{text:"Jump over it",correct:!1},{text:"Ignore it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every worker is responsible for keeping their own work area clean and safe, so that no one falls."},{question:"How should walkways and exits be kept?",options:[{text:"Open and free of obstructions",correct:!0},{text:"Used to store boxes when the store is full",correct:!1},{text:"Blocked only for short periods during work",correct:!1},{text:"Cleared only before an inspection visit",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keep walkways and exits open and free of obstructions; many accidents happen because of slipping and tripping over scattered materials."},{question:"You have finished a job. What do you do with the tools you used?",options:[{text:"Leave them on the floor for the next shift",correct:!1},{text:"Return each tool to its place",correct:!0},{text:"Put them in the waste bin with the scraps",correct:!1},{text:"Pile them in the walkway near the exit",correct:!1}],explanation:"Return every tool to its place when the work is finished and collect waste in designated bins; scattered materials cause slips and trips."}]},{id:17,code:"QUAK-117",icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes in factories",subtitle:"Earthquake safety in factories",summary:"Worker safety during and after an earthquake",blocks:[{body:"An earthquake creates a double hazard in a factory: the shaking itself, plus falling heavy machinery, chemical spills, broken pipes and gas leaks. Every factory must have an earthquake plan."},{heading:"1. Drop, Cover, Hold On",body:"During an earthquake:",points:["Drop to the ground (Drop) and get under a sturdy table or workbench — away from heavy machinery, shelving and glass.","If there is no table, kneel next to an interior wall and cover your head and neck with your hands (Cover).","Hold on to the table (Hold On) until the shaking stops completely.","Do not run, and do not go under machinery or next to shelving! Heavy machinery and shelving can shift or fall on you."]},{heading:"2. After the earthquake",body:"Once the shaking stops:",points:["Inspect machinery for damage, and do not switch on damaged machinery until it has been inspected.","Check whether any chemicals have spilled, and keep away from them.","If you smell gas — get out quickly, evacuate the factory and call 115 from outside.","Only trained, designated staff shut off the gas, and only if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound and the gas valve can be reached safely, without entering the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; shut off the electricity if wiring or electrical panels have been damaged.","Before going back inside, an engineer must inspect the factory building for cracks."]},{heading:"3. Preparedness",body:"Planning ahead:",points:["Prepare an earthquake plan for every department.","Secure heavy machinery with chains or anchor bolts.","Store drums and chemical containers on low, stable shelving.","Keep exit routes open at all times.","Hold a drill once every 6 months."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/factory_earthquake.png",title:"Earthquake",description:"An earthquake in a factory"}],quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake in a factory, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Run outside",correct:!1},{text:"Drop to the ground and get under a sturdy table",correct:!0},{text:"Switch off the machines",correct:!1},{text:"Go near the windows",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drop, Cover, Hold On is the first action inside buildings, away from machinery, shelving and glass."},{question:"After an earthquake in a chemical plant, what is the first thing to check?",options:[{text:"Electricity",correct:!1},{text:"Chemical spills",correct:!0},{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"Telephones",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chemical spills are a major hazard after an earthquake and must be checked quickly."},{question:"After the shaking stops, you smell gas in the factory. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Go to the gas valve and shut it off yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the extractor fans to clear the gas",correct:!1},{text:"Get out quickly and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Call 115 on your phone first, then leave",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you smell gas, get out quickly, evacuate the factory and call 115 from outside; only trained, designated staff shut off the gas, and only if the valve can be reached safely without entering the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark."}]},{id:18,code:"FLOOD-118",icon:"Waves",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Floods and water inundation",subtitle:"Flood emergency in industrial areas",summary:"Protecting the factory and its workers during a flood",blocks:[{body:"A flood can completely destroy a factory: it can ruin machinery, spread chemicals and turn the electrical system into a hazard. Factories near rivers and valleys are at greater risk."},{heading:"1. Before the flood",body:"Preparedness:",points:["Follow warnings from the meteorological service.","Move important equipment and chemicals to a higher location.","Prepare the electrical system for automatic shutoff.","Place sandbags in front of the doors and along the foundation of the factory."]},{heading:"2. During the flood",body:"Emergency procedures:",points:["If you can do so safely and the water has not reached the electrical panels, shut off the electricity and gas; never touch anything electrical while standing in water!","Do not enter flood water — 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away.","Move to higher ground — the second floor or the roof.","Call 115 and stay where you are until the rescue teams arrive."]},{heading:"3. After the flood",body:"Returning and cleaning up:",points:["Do not enter the factory until an engineer confirms that the building is safe.","Do not touch chemicals that have been in contact with flood water — request a hazardous materials (HazMat) team through 115.","Do not switch the power back on until the system has been inspected by a qualified electrician.","Photograph all damage for insurance and reporting."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/factory_flood.png",title:"Flood",description:"A flood in a factory"}],quiz:[{question:"During a flood in a factory, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Protect the machinery",correct:!1},{text:"Shut off the electricity and gas, if you can do so safely",correct:!0},{text:"Go home",correct:!1},{text:"Pump out the water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Electricity and gas are the primary hazards during a flood; however, if the water has reached the electrical panels, do not approach them and move to higher ground."},{question:"How much fast-moving water can be enough to knock a person down?",options:[{text:"50 cm",correct:!1},{text:"75 cm",correct:!1},{text:"15 cm",correct:!0},{text:"1 meter",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not enter flood water: 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away. Move to higher ground and call 115."},{question:"After the flood, you find chemical drums that were in the flood water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Rinse them with clean water and put them back on the shelf",correct:!1},{text:"Do not touch them; ask 115 for a HazMat team",correct:!0},{text:"Carry them outside by hand before the water rises again",correct:!1},{text:"Open them to check whether water got inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not touch chemicals that have been in contact with flood water; request a hazardous materials (HazMat) team through 115."}]},{id:19,code:"FACT-119",icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Civil Defense response in factories",subtitle:"Industrial Civil Defense response and multi-agency coordination",summary:"Organizing the factory response team during incidents and coordinating with 115",blocks:[{body:"Large facilities (oil, gas, chemicals, steel) must have their own emergency response teams that act before the 115 teams arrive. The NFPA 600 standard (Facility Fire Brigades) sets out the organization, training and equipment of facility fire brigades. The first minutes of an incident are critical, so the facility team must be ready and trained."},{heading:"1. Organizing the facility emergency response team (ERT)",body:"Key principles of the emergency response team:",points:["Team size: determined by the facility hazard assessment and the team's assigned duties; NFPA 600 does not set a fixed ratio of members to the number of employees.","Training: based on each member's duties, with refresher training at least once a year.","Incident Commander (IC): only one person gives orders, so that instructions are not mixed up.","Main duties: firefighting, rescue, first aid and building evacuation.","Communications: two-way radio on a dedicated channel, with cell phones as a backup.","Response time: define it in the plan, measure it during drills and improve it.","Recommended: the ICS-100 and ICS-200 courses (FEMA) to learn the Incident Command System."]},{heading:"2. The emergency response plan (ERP)",body:"Requirements for the emergency response plan under the standards:",points:["The emergency action plan must be in writing and available at the workplace; employers with 10 or fewer employees may communicate it orally (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38).","Main sections: roles, procedures, exit routes, communications, resources, drills and post-incident review.","Assembly points: one primary and one alternate, in a safe location away from the building and from the emergency vehicle access route.","Exit routes: in general, at least 2 exits from every floor (NFPA 101, with some exceptions).","Fire alarm: must be audible or visible in all areas of the building (NFPA 72).","First aid team: every shift must have employees trained in first aid and CPR.","Drills: held regularly, with the results of every drill recorded.","Post-incident review (Post-Response Critique): as soon as possible after every incident."]},{heading:"3. Coordination with Civil Defense (115)",body:"When an incident occurs and the 115 teams arrive, the ERT must take these steps:",points:["1. Call 115 immediately, and 122 if there are casualties.","2. Give the key information: location, type of incident, number of casualties, hazardous materials involved and the access route.","3. Have the site map ready at the gate, showing hazard locations and the electricity and gas shutoff points.","4. Liaison officer: only one person communicates with 115.","5. Have the SDS ready for every chemical on site.","6. Record the names and statements of witnesses.","7. Transfer of command: when 115 arrives, command passes to the 115 commander and the ERT provides support.","8. Documentation: photos, video and the incident log for investigation and insurance."]},{heading:"4. Working with the hazardous materials (HazMat) team",body:"For chemical incidents, a HazMat team is required:",points:["HazMat training levels (OSHA 1910.120 and NFPA 470, which consolidates NFPA 472):","• Awareness level: recognizing the hazard, staying away and raising the alarm — for all employees.","• Operations level: protecting people and the surroundings with defensive actions from a distance.","• Technician level: offensive actions to stop the leak at close range, with specialized PPE.","• Specialist level: in-depth knowledge of specific substances and technical support for technicians.","Three zones: the hot zone, the warm zone and the cold zone.","HazMat PPE levels: A, B, C, D (A gives the highest protection).","Decontamination is carried out in the warm zone, before entering the cold zone.","Untrained employees do not enter the hot zone; they wait for the 115 teams.","The facility plan must state how specialized hazardous materials teams are requested through 115."]},{heading:"5. Multi-agency response",body:"Major incidents require several agencies:",points:["115 Civil Defense: firefighting, rescue and hazardous materials.","122 Ambulance: medical treatment and transport of casualties.","104 Police: cordoning off the area, traffic control and legal investigation.","Security agencies: when explosives or terrorism are suspected — notified through 104.","Ministry of Health: preparing nearby hospitals.","Electricity directorate: cutting off power in the surrounding area.","Water and gas authorities: shutting off water and gas lines.","Environmental authority: if chemicals have been released into the environment.","Emergency Operations Center (EOC): for major incidents involving many agencies."]},{heading:"6. Phases of response (5 phases)",body:"Every incident goes through these 5 phases:",points:["1. Detection: sensors, the fire alarm or a witness.","2. Notification: calling 115, and alerting the ERT and the liaison officer.","3. Response: the ERT acts until the 115 teams arrive.","4. Mitigation: firefighting, rescue and decontamination.","5. Recovery: cleanup, analysis and post-incident review.","Every phase must be documented in an Incident Action Plan (IAP).","The Golden Hour: rapid treatment after a serious injury increases the chance of survival — which is why calling 122 quickly matters."]},{heading:"7. Drills and training",body:"Drills are the foundation of preparedness (recommended schedule):",points:["Tabletop exercise: once every 3 months — a scenario worked through around a table.","Functional drill: once every 6 months — the ERT only, not the whole building.","Full-scale drill: once a year — the entire facility together with 115.","Evaluation: an after-action report as soon as possible.","ERT training: ongoing and based on assigned duties, at least once a year.","Competency standards: NFPA 1081 (Facility Fire Brigade Member), OSHA 1910 Subpart L and ICS-100/200.","Joint drill with 115: once a year for coordination."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_ert_response.mp4",title:"ERT response drill",description:"A full-scale drill at an oil facility"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_ics_chart.png",title:"Incident Command System (ICS) structure",description:"Incident Command System (ICS) structure"}],quiz:[{question:"How is the size of a facility emergency response team (ERT) determined?",options:[{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 per 100 employees",correct:!1},{text:"Based on the facility hazard assessment and the team's assigned duties",correct:!0},{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 per 200 employees",correct:!1},{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 per 25 employees",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 600 does not set a fixed ratio of members to the number of employees; each facility determines the team size according to its own hazards and duties."},{question:"What does the Golden Hour mean?",options:[{text:"A special working hour",correct:!1},{text:"The initial period after a serious injury, when rapid treatment is critical",correct:!0},{text:"Midday",correct:!1},{text:"The heat of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Golden Hour is the period after a serious injury during which getting the casualty to treatment quickly increases the chance of survival; so call 122 immediately."},{question:"In the ICS, what does the liaison officer do?",options:[{text:"Puts out the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Communicates with outside agencies",correct:!0},{text:"Performs CPR",correct:!1},{text:"Guards the gate",correct:!1}],explanation:"The liaison officer communicates with 115, the police (104), the ambulance service (122) and other agencies."},{question:"What does the HazMat Operations level do?",options:[{text:"Raises the alarm only",correct:!1},{text:"Defensive — preventing the spread from a distance",correct:!0},{text:"Offensive — stopping the leak at close range",correct:!1},{text:"Specialist",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Operations level is defensive: preventing the spread of the substance from a distance, not offensive work at close range."},{question:"What is the hot zone in a HazMat incident?",options:[{text:"A cool, safe place",correct:!1},{text:"A completely clean zone",correct:!1},{text:"The most dangerous zone — HazMat technicians only, with appropriate PPE",correct:!0},{text:"The assembly area",correct:!1}],explanation:"The hot zone is where the hazardous material is; only HazMat technicians in Level A or B PPE enter it."},{question:"When the 115 teams arrive at the incident scene, who takes command?",options:[{text:"The ERT remains in command",correct:!1},{text:"The 115 commander",correct:!0},{text:"The factory manager",correct:!1},{text:"The police",correct:!1}],explanation:"Transfer of command (ICS Transfer): when 115 arrives, command passes to the 115 commander and the ERT provides support."}]},{id:20,code:"FACT-120",icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire classification in factories",subtitle:"Industrial fire classes — NFPA 30/484/654",summary:"Classification specific to industry: flammable liquids, gases, metals and combustible dust",blocks:[{body:"This module uses the NFPA system: A ordinary solids, B flammable liquids and gases, C electrical equipment, D metals, K cooking oil. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Industry-specific standards: flammable liquids (NFPA 30), combustible metals (NFPA 484) and combustible dust (NFPA 654). Since the 2025 edition, NFPA 484 and NFPA 654 have been consolidated into the NFPA 660 standard."},{heading:"1. Class A — combustible solids in the factory",body:"Common combustible solids in factories:",points:["Fuels: paper, cardboard, fabric, wood, rubber, plastic and pallets.","These materials leave embers and hidden fire behind; after extinguishing, cool the area with water and keep it under watch.","In factories: paper and cardboard storage areas and wooden pallets spread fire quickly.","Extinguisher: water, foam, ABC powder.","Stacks of empty pallets are a major hazard: do not stack them high, and store them outdoors or in a sprinklered area.","Standard: NFPA 13 (sprinkler systems), which also covers storage protection.","Examples in factories: warehouses and packaging lines."]},{heading:"2. Class B — flammable liquids in the factory",body:"One of the most dangerous classes in factories:",points:["Fuels: gasoline, kerosene, diesel, acetone, toluene, xylene.","Flash point: gasoline about -43°C, acetone -20°C, diesel 52°C or higher.","Explosive limits (LEL/UEL): the minimum and maximum vapor concentrations in air that can ignite:","• Gasoline: 1.4-7.6%.","• Acetone: 2.5-12.8%.","• Methanol: 6-36%.","Extinguisher: foam (AFFF or fluorine-free foam), CO₂, ABC powder. For acetone, methanol, alcohols and other polar solvents: CO₂, dry chemical or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them.","Do not direct a water jet onto a burning liquid — it spreads the fire. A BLEVE (Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion) occurs when a pressurized tank is heated by fire.","Liquid classes under NFPA 30 (based on flash point and boiling point):","• Class I (Flammable): flash point below 37.8°C — IA (flash point < 22.8°C and boiling point < 37.8°C), IB (flash point < 22.8°C and boiling point 37.8°C or higher), IC (flash point 22.8 to < 37.8°C).","• Class II (Combustible): flash point 37.8 to < 60°C.","• Class III (Combustible): IIIA from 60 to < 93°C, and IIIB 93°C or higher.","Secondary containment (diking): under NFPA 30, the volume of the containment area must be no less than the liquid that can be released from the largest full tank (100%); a 110% capacity is required by some other regulations and guidelines (to allow for rainwater as well).","Vapor density: gasoline vapor is heavier than air (about 3-4 times) and collects in low-lying areas."]},{heading:"3. Flammable gases — NFPA: Class B, EN 2: Class C",body:"Industrial gases: acetylene, propane, hydrogen:",points:["Sources: acetylene for welding, propane for some forklifts, hydrogen from charging lead-acid batteries.","These substances are gases at normal temperatures and ignite easily. Explosive limits in air: hydrogen 4-75%, propane 2.1-9.5%, acetylene from 2.5% upward. (-253°C is the boiling point of hydrogen, not its flash point.)","The temperature of an oxy-acetylene flame exceeds 3000°C.","Acetylene forms an explosive compound (copper acetylide) with copper; do not use unsuitable copper pipes or fittings.","Extinguishing: first, if it is safe to do so, close the gas valve. Do not extinguish a gas fire if you cannot stop the gas flow — the leaking gas can cause an explosion.","Civil Defense teams (115) use water spray from a distance to cool cylinders and tanks, not to extinguish the gas flame.","Gas detectors in gas storage areas: the alarm must sound well before the LEL is reached (typically at 10-20% of the LEL).","In factories: welding areas, forklift charging areas and battery rooms."]},{heading:"4. Class D — combustible metals (NFPA 484)",body:"Combustible metals — one of the most dangerous types:",points:["Fuels: magnesium, titanium, aluminum powder, sodium and lithium metal.","These metals burn at extremely high temperatures and give off very intense light; do not look directly at the flame.","Water is prohibited! Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ (hydrogen is produced and can explode).","CO₂ is prohibited! 2Mg + CO₂ → 2MgO + C (magnesium even burns in CO₂).","Class D extinguishers only, and only one whose label lists that metal: the agent differs from metal to metal (for example sodium chloride (NaCl)-based powder or graphite; copper powder for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's SDS names it and it is completely dry.","Examples in factories: aluminum smelting, magnesium machining and lithium-metal batteries.","NFPA 484: storage, handling and protection of combustible metals (now part of NFPA 660).","Aluminum powder: also carries a risk of dust explosion (NFPA 654 / 660).","Response: call 115 immediately and use only a Class D extinguisher listed for that metal. Lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries contain no metallic lithium and are cooled with water."]},{heading:"5. Combustible dust — dust explosions (NFPA 654)",body:"A deadly hazard in factories:",points:["The dust explosion pentagon: fuel (dust) + oxygen + heat (ignition) + dispersion in air + confinement.","Sources: flour, sugar, wood, coal, metal dust and plastics.","The minimum ignition temperature (MIT) varies with the type of dust; refer to the SDS or the dust test results.","The minimum explosible concentration (MEC) also varies with the type of dust; dust dispersed in the air is always dangerous.","Primary explosion: inside a piece of equipment (such as a filter or a mill).","Secondary explosion: the primary explosion lifts dust from floors, shelves and pipes, causing a larger and more deadly explosion.","Risk reduction: dust collection systems, explosion vents and suppression systems.","Housekeeping: a dust layer 0.8 mm (1/32 inch) thick over more than 5% of the floor area can create a hazard (NFPA 654 / OSHA).","Example: in 2008 at the Imperial Sugar refinery (USA), a sugar dust explosion killed 14 workers (CSB).","Standards: NFPA 654 (now NFPA 660) and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.272 (for grain handling facilities)."]},{heading:"6. Stages of fire development in a factory",body:"The 4 standard stages — in factories a fire can develop faster:",points:["Incipient: a small fire — a trained person may put it out with the right portable extinguisher, after the alarm is raised and 115 called, with the exit route behind them; if it does not go out at once, get out.","Growth: fire and smoke increase rapidly and sprinklers activate — get out and call 115.","Fully developed: after flashover — Civil Defense teams (115) only.","Decay: the fuel decreases, but the risk of backdraft and collapse remains.","In factories, high-rack storage, pallets and liquids can bring a fire to the fully developed stage faster than in homes.","Pressurized gas and liquid tanks heated by fire can explode as a BLEVE — evacuate the surrounding area quickly.","The factory plan must keep the access route for 115 teams and the fire hydrant locations ready and unobstructed.","Fire watch after hot work: during the work and for at least 1 hour after it is finished, then monitoring for up to 3 more hours as decided by the permit authorizing individual (NFPA 51B)."]},{heading:"7. Fire load and risk assessment",body:"Assessing the hazard in the factory:",points:["Fire load density (MJ/m²): the amount of energy that combustible materials can release per square meter. Average values from EN 1991-1-2 (Eurocode 1):","• Dwelling: about 780 MJ/m².","• Office: about 420 MJ/m².","• Library: about 1500 MJ/m².","• Warehouses and factories: calculated from the materials stored, and often higher than these values.","Risk assessment: risk = likelihood of occurrence × severity of the consequences.","• Low: controlled by normal procedures.","• Medium: requires additional controls and monitoring.","• High: requires urgent action before work continues.","• Extreme: work stops until the risk is reduced.","The fire resistance rating of walls and roofs is set by building codes according to the type and use of the building (such as NFPA 101 and NFPA 5000).","Update the facility risk assessment (such as an HVA) at least once a year and after every major change."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_dust_explosion.mp4",title:"Dust explosion",description:"A dust explosion demonstration"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_fire_classes.png",title:"Fire class chart",description:"Fire classification in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for a Class D fire?",options:[{text:"ABC powder",correct:!1},{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"A Class D extinguisher listed for that metal",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists the burning metal is suitable; the agent differs from metal to metal. Do not use water, CO₂ or ABC powder."},{question:"What does BLEVE stand for?",options:[{text:"A fire alarm",correct:!1},{text:"Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion",correct:!0},{text:"A sprinkler system",correct:!1},{text:"A class of gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"A BLEVE is the explosion of a pressurized liquid tank when it is heated by fire and the vapor expands rapidly."},{question:"How many elements does the dust explosion pentagon have?",options:[{text:"3",correct:!1},{text:"4",correct:!1},{text:"5",correct:!0},{text:"6",correct:!1}],explanation:"Five elements: fuel + oxygen + heat + dispersion + confinement."},{question:"What does acetylene form with copper?",options:[{text:"Nothing happens",correct:!1},{text:"Copper acetylide — an explosive compound",correct:!0},{text:"Cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"A small amount of heat",correct:!1}],explanation:"Acetylene forms a sensitive explosive compound with copper (copper acetylide) that detonates on impact or heating."},{question:"What hazard does aluminum powder present?",options:[{text:"Class A only",correct:!1},{text:"Class D and dust explosion",correct:!0},{text:"Gas only",correct:!1},{text:"No hazard",correct:!1}],explanation:"Aluminum powder is both a combustible metal (Class D) and a combustible dust (NFPA 484 / 654, now NFPA 660)."},{question:"According to EN 1991-1-2, what is the approximate average fire load in dwellings?",options:[{text:"100 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"230 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"420 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"780 MJ/m²",correct:!0}],explanation:"The average fire load in dwellings is about 780 MJ/m², and in offices about 420 MJ/m² (EN 1991-1-2, Table E.4)."}]},{id:21,code:"FACT-121",icon:"ShowerHead",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Fire suppression systems in factories",subtitle:"Portable extinguishers + fixed systems (sprinkler/foam/CO₂)",summary:"Portable extinguishers and fixed fire suppression systems in factories",blocks:[{body:"Factories need two levels of fire suppression: 1) portable extinguishers for small fires in their early stage, 2) fixed suppression systems for continuous protection. Depending on the type of hazard: sprinklers (NFPA 13), foam (NFPA 11 / 16), CO₂ (NFPA 12), dry chemical (NFPA 17) and clean agent (NFPA 2001). This module explains these systems and the inspection schedule."},{heading:"1. Portable extinguishers — selection for the factory",body:"The 7 main types and a selection guide:",points:["ABC powder (multipurpose): the most common type for corridors and general areas — A, B, C (not D or K).","CO₂: for electrical panels and server rooms — B, C.","Foam (AFFF): for flammable liquid storage and diked areas — A, B.","Wet chemical: for the factory kitchen and cafeteria — K.","Clean agent: for server and control rooms.","Class D (agent listed on the label for the metal in use): for metal machining and aluminum smelting.","Water mist (fine deionized water): for A, and some models for C — according to the device listing.","Hazard levels under NFPA 10 (minimum Class A extinguisher rating):","• Light hazard (offices): 2-A.","• Ordinary hazard (typical factory): 2-A.","• Extra hazard (factory with large amounts of combustibles): 4-A.","Travel distance to an extinguisher:","• Class A: up to 23 meters (75 feet) at all hazard levels.","• Class B: 9 or 15 meters (30 / 50 feet) depending on the extinguisher rating and the hazard level."]},{heading:"2. Sprinkler systems — NFPA 13",body:"The most important fixed system in many factories:",points:["The 4 types of sprinkler system:","• Wet pipe: the pipes are always full of water — the fastest response and the most common type.","• Dry pipe: the pipes are filled with pressurized air or nitrogen — for areas where the temperature drops below 4°C.","• Pre-action: the valve opens only after detectors sense a fire, then the sprinkler head opens from the heat — for areas with sensitive equipment.","• Deluge: open heads that all discharge water at once — for high-hazard areas.","Activation temperature and glass bulb color:","• 57°C (orange): ordinary temperature rating — areas with low ambient temperatures.","• 68°C (red): ordinary temperature rating — the most common in offices and factories.","• 79°C (yellow) and 93°C (green): intermediate temperature rating — warmer areas.","• 141°C (blue): high temperature rating — boiler rooms and near hot equipment.","Coverage area per standard spray head: up to about 21 m² (light hazard), 12 m² (ordinary hazard) and 9 m² (extra hazard).","Water density: 0.10-0.40 gpm/ft² (about 4-16 mm/min) depending on the hazard level.","NFPA 25: inspection and testing on weekly, monthly, annual and 5-year schedules.","Sprinklers open only where the fire is; the heads do not all open at once (except in a deluge system)."]},{heading:"3. Foam systems — NFPA 11 / 16",body:"For large flammable liquid storage facilities:",points:["The 3 types of foam by expansion ratio:","• Low expansion (up to 20:1): tank fires and liquid spills.","• Medium expansion (20:1 to 200:1): diked areas.","• High expansion (200:1 to 1000:1): aircraft hangars and large enclosed spaces.","Types of foam concentrate:","• AFFF: for hydrocarbons (gasoline, diesel).","• AR-AFFF (Alcohol-Resistant): for polar solvents (alcohol, acetone).","• Fluoroprotein: for storage tanks.","• Fluorine-free foam (F3): the replacement for PFAS foams.","Application rate:","• Hydrocarbon spills: a minimum of about 4.1 L/min/m² (0.10 gpm/ft²).","• Polar solvents: according to the manufacturer's listing, generally higher.","PFAS hazard: AFFF contains fluorinated substances (PFAS) that accumulate in the body and the environment; many countries are moving to fluorine-free foam (F3).","In industry: tank farms, tanker loading racks and refineries."]},{heading:"4. CO₂ and clean agent systems — NFPA 12 / 2001",body:"For equipment rooms and total flooding:",points:["Total flooding with CO₂:","• Design concentration: a minimum of 34% (NFPA 12), and higher for some materials.","• Hazard: the extinguishing concentration is lethal to people — no one may remain in the room.","• Pre-discharge alarm and time delay: must be long enough to evacuate the room before the gas is released (NFPA 12).","• Applications: generator rooms, engine test cells and printing presses.","Clean agent (FK-5-1-12 / HFC-227ea):","• NOAEL: in occupied spaces, the design concentration does not exceed the no-observed-adverse-effect level (NOAEL) (NFPA 2001); even so, evacuate the room.","• Design concentration: generally about 4-9% depending on the agent.","• Discharge: halocarbon agents within 10 seconds (NFPA 2001).","• Applications: server rooms, control rooms and switchgear rooms.","• It costs much more than water-based systems.","Halon 1301: under the Montreal Protocol, its production has been halted in developed countries since 1994 because it depletes the ozone layer; new systems are not installed with it.","Hydrostatic testing of portable extinguishers (NFPA 10): CO₂ every 5 years, clean agent every 12 years; fixed-system cylinders according to NFPA 12 / 2001 and cylinder regulations."]},{heading:"5. Dry chemical systems — NFPA 17",body:"For special hazards:",points:["Pre-engineered systems:","• Commercial kitchens: wet chemical systems (NFPA 17A) are now generally used, not dry chemical.","• Paint spray booths.","• Protection of heavy equipment and vehicles.","• Dip tanks.","Agents:","• Sodium bicarbonate: B, C.","• Potassium bicarbonate (Purple-K): B, C — more effective than sodium bicarbonate on Class B fires.","• Monoammonium phosphate: A, B, C.","• Potassium chloride: B, C.","Activation: a heat detector or a manual pull station.","After discharge: the powder spreads everywhere and can damage electronic equipment; cleanup is difficult.","The coverage area of each nozzle is set by the system listing."]},{heading:"6. Fire detection — NFPA 72",body:"The detection system — the first line of warning:",points:["Types of detectors:","• Smoke: photoelectric for smoldering fires, ionization for flaming fires.","• Heat: fixed temperature (for example 57°C) or rate-of-rise.","• Flame: UV/IR detectors, which detect flames quickly.","• Gas: LEL detectors and CO sensors.","• Air sampling: for server rooms — the most sensitive type.","Spacing and coverage:","• Smoke: spacing between detectors of about 9 meters (30 ft) on a smooth ceiling (NFPA 72).","• Heat: according to the detector's listed spacing.","• Flame: according to the device's field of view and listed distance.","Alarm notification:","• Audible: generally at least 15 dB above the average ambient sound level (NFPA 72).","• Visual (strobe): for noisy areas and for people with hearing impairments.","• Voice / mass notification: for large buildings and facilities.","Inspection and testing: according to the NFPA 72 schedules (many devices are tested once a year)."]},{heading:"7. Inspection and maintenance schedule",body:"According to NFPA standards:",points:["Daily / weekly:","• Visual inspection (gauge pressure, damage).","• Check that nothing is obstructing the sprinklers.","• Running the fire pump without water flow (no-flow test): diesel pumps every week, electric pumps generally every month (NFPA 25).","Monthly:","• Visual inspection of portable extinguishers (NFPA 10).","• Inspection of the fire alarm panel and trouble signals.","• Inspection of manual pull stations — visible and unobstructed.","Annually:","• Annual maintenance of portable extinguishers by a qualified person (not hydrostatic testing).","• Inspection of sprinklers from floor level (NFPA 25).","• A sample of foam concentrate sent for testing.","• Testing of detectors and alarms (NFPA 72).","Every few years:","• Hydrostatic test of extinguishers (pressure test of the cylinder, NFPA 10): every 5 years for water, foam, wet chemical, CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical units; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical units with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells, and for clean agent units. Separately, every 6 years, stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need the 12-year test are emptied and checked inside by a qualified person. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.","• Internal inspection of sprinkler piping: every 5 years (NFPA 25).","• Replacement or sample testing of sprinkler heads after 50 years (standard heads — NFPA 25).","Recordkeeping: document every inspection and test, and keep the records as required by the standards and the relevant authorities."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_sprinkler_demo.mp4",title:"Sprinkler activation",description:"A sprinkler demonstration"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_systems_chart.png",title:"Suppression systems chart",description:"Fixed systems"}],quiz:[{question:"For most factories, which is the most important fixed system?",options:[{text:"Portable extinguishers",correct:!1},{text:"Sprinklers (NFPA 13)",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂ only",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"Sprinklers operate automatically and control a fire in its early stage, even when no one is on site; portable extinguishers are only for small fires."},{question:"Where is a wet pipe system not suitable?",options:[{text:"Equipment rooms (ICT)",correct:!1},{text:"Offices",correct:!1},{text:"Areas where the temperature drops below 4°C",correct:!0},{text:"Warehouses",correct:!1}],explanation:"In cold areas the water in the pipes freezes; a dry pipe system is suitable."},{question:"Are new systems installed with Halon 1301?",options:[{text:"Yes",correct:!1},{text:"No — its production has been halted under the Montreal Protocol",correct:!0},{text:"Only in ICT rooms",correct:!1},{text:"Only in server rooms",correct:!1}],explanation:"Halon 1301 depletes the ozone layer; its production in developed countries has been halted since 1994."},{question:"Why must everyone leave the room before a total flooding CO₂ system discharges?",options:[{text:"It is not necessary; CO₂ is harmless",correct:!1},{text:"Because the extinguishing concentration (34% and above) is lethal to people",correct:!0},{text:"Because CO₂ sprays water",correct:!1},{text:"Because CO₂ makes a loud noise",correct:!1}],explanation:"The CO₂ design concentration in total flooding systems is at least 34% (NFPA 12), and this concentration causes unconsciousness and death within a short time; that is why there is a pre-discharge alarm and a time delay."},{question:"What is the approximate coverage area per standard spray sprinkler head?",options:[{text:"2-5 m²",correct:!1},{text:"12-21 m²",correct:!0},{text:"50 m²",correct:!1},{text:"100 m²",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NFPA 13: about 21 m² for light hazard and 12 m² for ordinary hazard; for extra hazard it is smaller (about 9 m²)."},{question:"What does the weekly test of a diesel fire pump involve?",options:[{text:"A visual inspection only",correct:!1},{text:"Starting the pump without water flow (no-flow test)",correct:!0},{text:"A hydrostatic test",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"NFPA 25: the diesel pump is started and tested without water flow every week, and the electric pump generally every month."}]},{id:22,code:"BUS-122",icon:"Building2",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Fire safety in shops, markets and malls",subtitle:"Exits, storage, electricity, LPG and staff roles in an emergency",summary:"Shops, markets and malls are full of customers who do not know the building, stock that burns easily, electrical equipment and often LPG cylinders. This lesson is for owners, managers and staff: how to keep exits and fire equipment clear, how to prevent electrical and gas fires, and what each member of staff does when the alarm sounds. Local licensing and inspection rules are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority; follow their requirements.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Exits, escape routes and storage",body:"Customers usually try to leave the way they came in, so every way out must be ready at all times:",points:["Keep exits, corridors and stairs completely clear: no stock, boxes, carts or stalls in the way, not even for a short time.","Exit doors must open from the inside at once, without a key or code, whenever people are inside; remove chains, bars and extra locks before opening time.","Keep exit signs visible: goods, posters and decorations must not hide an exit door or its sign.","Keep extra stock in a storeroom, not in corridors, under stairs or in areas where customers walk.","Do not pile goods, cardboard or packaging against electrical panels, lights, heaters or cooking equipment.","In a market or mall, agree with the management on the shared exits, the alarm and the assembly point, and take part in joint drills."]},{heading:"2. Electricity: overloads and extension leads",body:"Overloaded sockets and cables that overheat are common causes of fire in shops:",points:["Do not overload sockets or power strips, and use power strips with built-in overload protection.","Extension leads are for temporary use only and never replace fixed wiring; if you need more sockets, have a qualified electrician install them.","Plug refrigerators, freezers, heaters, ovens and other large appliances directly into a wall socket, never into an extension lead.","Do not run cables under carpets or mats, through doorways or across walkways, where they can be crushed or make people trip.","Replace damaged cables and plugs at once. A burning smell, a hot plug, sparks or a breaker that keeps tripping means: switch off and call a qualified electrician."]},{heading:"3. LPG cylinders in shops and markets",body:"LPG is heavier than air: leaking gas collects near the floor and in low places such as drains and basements.",points:["Keep in the shop only the smallest number of cylinders the business needs; keep the rest in a separate, ventilated store, preferably in the open air.","Never keep cylinders below ground level, in a basement or next to drains.","Keep cylinders upright with the valve protected, where customers cannot touch them, away from heat, flames and things that burn.","Never keep cylinders on stairs, beside exit doors or anywhere they block the way out.","Only a qualified technician connects regulators, hoses and appliances. Never look for a leak with a flame.","How many cylinders a shop may keep, and where, is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]},{heading:"4. If you smell gas",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights, appliances or machines on or off. Do not use a phone inside.
2. Stop sales and get customers and staff out calmly through the nearest exit.
3. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
4. Keep people away from the entrance and warn the neighboring shops.
5. Call 115 from outside, and do not let anyone back in until the specialists say it is safe.`,critical:!0},{heading:"5. Staff roles when the alarm sounds",body:"Every member of staff knows their role before an emergency. The emergency plan names a person for each task on every shift:",points:['Raise the alarm: shout "Fire!", press the nearest fire alarm call point if there is one, and make sure someone calls 115 from a safe place.',"Guide customers: stop sales, tell customers calmly to leave and point them to the nearest exit, not only to the main entrance.","Check: look into toilets, fitting rooms, storerooms and offices on your way out, only if it is safe, and close doors behind you.","Help: assist those who need it, such as older people, children who are alone and people with disabilities. Never use the elevators.","At the assembly point: count the staff, report anyone missing, and tell the firefighters where people may still be and about gas cylinders and other dangers.","Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe."]},{heading:"6. Getting a crowd out safely",body:"In a busy shop or mall, the way staff guide people decides how quickly everyone gets out:",points:['Speak calmly and give short, clear instructions, such as: "Leave by this exit. Walk, do not run."',"Use every available exit, and send people to another exit if one becomes crowded or smoke blocks it.","Do not let people stop to pay, collect goods or go back inside.","Keep the number of people inside within the limit set for the building, especially on holidays and sale days.","In a mall, follow the instructions of the mall's emergency team and the announcements, and move well away from the building to the assembly point."]},{heading:"7. Fire equipment, first-aid kit and AED",body:"Equipment saves lives only if people can see it, reach it and use it:",points:["Keep fire extinguishers, fire alarm call points and fire blankets visible, in their place and unobstructed; check every day that nothing is in front of them.","Use an extinguisher only if you are trained, the fire is small and just starting, the label shows that it suits this fire, others have been warned, 115 has been called and your exit is behind you. Otherwise get out and close the door.","Each extinguisher needs a visual check every month (in its place, safety pin and tamper seal intact, pressure gauge in the green, no damage) and servicing by a qualified person; the local schedule is set by Civil Defense.","Keep a complete first-aid kit where staff can reach it quickly, and know who on each shift is trained in first aid.","If the shop or mall has an AED, every member of staff knows where it is. If someone collapses and is not breathing normally: call 122, start CPR and send someone to bring the AED at once."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_shop_evacuation.webp",title:"Evacuating a shop",description:"A staff member guides customers to the nearest exit while a colleague checks the storeroom"}],quiz:[{question:"The storeroom is full and a delivery arrives. Where may the extra boxes go?",options:[{text:"In the corridor to the fire exit, until the end of the day",correct:!1},{text:"Under the stairs, as long as customers cannot see them",correct:!1},{text:"In another storeroom or a place away from exits and walkways",correct:!0},{text:"In front of the fire extinguisher, where there is floor space",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exits, corridors and stairs must stay clear at all times, even for a short time, and nothing may block fire equipment. Extra stock goes in a storeroom away from exits and walkways."},{question:"A new display refrigerator needs power. What is the safe way to connect it?",options:[{text:"Plug it into the power strip that already feeds the lights and the cash register",correct:!1},{text:"Plug it into a wall socket; an electrician adds more sockets if needed",correct:!0},{text:"Run an extension lead to it under the floor mat so nobody trips over it",correct:!1},{text:"Join two extension leads together to reach the socket by the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Large appliances go directly into a wall socket. Extension leads are temporary and overload easily, and a cable under a mat can be crushed and overheat."},{question:"Staff smell gas strongly near the shop's LPG cylinders, and the valve is in the middle of the smell. What is right?",options:[{text:"Walk to the valve, close it and switch on the fan to clear the gas",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the lights to find the leak and test the hose with a lighter",correct:!1},{text:"Phone the gas supplier from inside the shop while looking for the leak",correct:!1},{text:"Stay away from the valve, get everyone out and call 115 from outside",correct:!0}],explanation:"The valve is closed only if it can be reached without going into the gas and without any spark. Here it cannot, so nobody goes near it: no switches, no phones inside, everyone out, and 115 is called from outside."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds in a busy shop. What does the staff member responsible for the toilets and storerooms do?",options:[{text:"Checks them on the way out if safe, then reports at the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"Stays at the cash register to finish the sales before leaving the building",correct:!1},{text:"Locks the storerooms so that nobody can take any goods during the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Goes back inside after the count to search for anyone who is missing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Checking toilets and storerooms on the way out, only if it is safe, makes sure nobody is left behind. The result is reported at the assembly point; nobody goes back inside."},{question:"During an evacuation, customers crowd at the main entrance while another exit is free. What do staff do?",options:[{text:"Tell everyone to wait in the shop until the main entrance is clear",correct:!1},{text:"Calmly direct people to the free exit with short, clear instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Open the elevators so that more people can leave the floor at once",correct:!1},{text:"Ask customers to pay quickly so that the line at the register moves faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"People tend to leave the way they came in. Staff use every available exit and calmly guide people to a free one; elevators are never used in a fire."}]},{id:23,code:"BUS-123",icon:"CookingPot",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Fire safety in restaurants and commercial kitchens",subtitle:"Oil and grease fires, hood systems, gas and staff drills",summary:"A commercial kitchen brings heat, oil, grease and gas together in a small space, and a fire in a pan or fryer can spread into the hood and its duct within moments. This lesson is for owners, managers, cooks and service staff: how to deal with an oil fire, what the kitchen hood fire suppression system does, how to handle gas safely, why grease must be cleaned and how to get customers out. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Oil and grease fires: never water",body:"Burning oil and fat need special handling:",points:["Never pour water on burning oil or fat: the water turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out in a fireball.","Do not carry or move a burning pan or fryer.","A small fire in a pan: turn off the heat or gas at the appliance only if you can reach it safely, then slide a metal lid over the pan or cover it with a fire blanket, and leave it covered until it has cooled.","An ABC powder or foam extinguisher is not suitable for a deep fryer: the oil stays hot and can flare up again.","If the fire is not small, reaches the hood or does not go out at once: get out, close the door and call 115."],critical:!0},{heading:"2. The wet chemical extinguisher (Class K / Class F)",body:"The project uses NFPA 10 letters as its reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. So Class K (NFPA) and Class F (EN 2) are the same class: cooking oils and fats.",points:["A wet chemical extinguisher is made for cooking oil and fat: it forms a soap-like layer on the oil that cuts off the air and cools it.","If the kitchen has a hood suppression system, activate the system first (automatically or with the manual pull station), and only then use the extinguisher. A sign next to the extinguisher says this.","Read the label before an emergency: the class, the operating range and how to use it. Some wet chemical units are operated differently from PASS; follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.","Use it only if you are trained, the fire is small, the alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you.","The color of an extinguisher is not its class: always read the label."]},{heading:"3. The kitchen hood fire suppression system",body:"Many commercial kitchens have a fixed fire suppression system in the hood over the fryers, grills and ranges. Staff do not service it, but everyone must know how it works:",points:["When a fire under the hood heats the system, it starts by itself and sprays an extinguishing agent from nozzles onto the cooking surfaces and into the hood and its duct.","When it is activated, it also shuts off the gas or electricity to the cooking equipment under the hood. Only a qualified person restores it afterward.","If it does not start by itself, use the manual pull station. It is on the way out of the kitchen, every cook must know where it is, and nothing may block it.","Keep the hood filters clean and in place. Keep the nozzles clean, and never cover, bend or move them.","Do not move, add or swap cooking appliances under the hood without the system installer checking it: the nozzles are aimed at specific appliances.","A qualified company inspects and services the system regularly and attaches a service tag; the interval is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]},{heading:"4. Gas cylinders, manifolds and pipework",body:"Gas in a kitchen is safe only when it is installed and handled correctly:",points:["Keep gas cylinders and their manifold (the pipe that joins several cylinders) outside the kitchen, in a ventilated place, upright and protected from heat and tampering; never in a basement or below ground.","Only a qualified gas technician installs, connects, repairs and services pipework, regulators and appliances. Staff change cylinders only if they have been trained to do it.","Train staff to look every day for obvious faults, such as a damaged hose or connection, a flame that goes out and does not stay lit or a smell of gas, and to report them and stop using the appliance.","Where the gas is interlocked with the extract fan, the gas stops when the fan stops. Never bypass or disable this interlock.","Every cook knows where the kitchen gas valve is, and uses it only as the gas-leak rule below allows.","Never look for a gas leak with a flame."]},{heading:"5. If you smell gas in the kitchen",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights, fans or appliances on or off. Do not use a phone inside.
2. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
3. Get staff and customers out calmly and warn the neighbors.
4. Call 115 from outside, and do not go back in until the specialists say it is safe.`,critical:!0},{heading:"6. Grease: clean the hood, filters and duct",body:"Grease that builds up in the hood, the filters and the extract duct is fuel waiting for a flame:",points:["Flames from frying, grilling or flambéing can set built-up grease alight, and a fire in the duct can spread through the building.","Clean filters, hood surfaces, walls, floors and appliances on a fixed schedule, so that grease does not build up.","Have the extract duct cleaned regularly by a qualified company and keep the record. How often depends on the type and amount of cooking; the requirement is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority.","Empty and clean a fryer only after the oil has cooled, and never move a fryer while the oil is hot.","Keep cardboard, cloths, paper and oil containers away from cooking equipment and heat."]},{heading:"7. Staff drills and getting customers out",body:"Everyone in the restaurant knows the plan from their first day at work:",points:["The emergency plan names who raises the alarm, who calls 115, who turns off the gas if it is safe, who guides customers and who counts staff at the assembly point.","Service staff calmly tell customers to leave by the nearest exit and do not let them stop to pay or collect belongings.","Check toilets, dining rooms and storerooms on the way out only if it is safe, and close doors behind you.","Keep the back door and the kitchen exit clear, and never lock exit doors while staff or customers are inside.","On their first day, show every new member of staff the exits, the pull station, the extinguisher, the fire blanket and the gas valve, and test the plan with drills.","At the assembly point, tell the firefighters whether the hood system has discharged, whether the gas is off and whether anyone is missing."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_kitchen_grease_fire.webp",title:"An oil fire in a commercial kitchen",description:"A cook slides a metal lid over a burning pan; the manual pull station and the wet chemical extinguisher are visible near the exit"}],quiz:[{question:"A small fire starts in a frying pan on the stove. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Pour a glass of water on the pan to cool the oil down quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the pan outside so that the fire is out of the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"If safe, turn off the heat and slide a metal lid over the pan",correct:!0},{text:"Spray the pan with the ABC powder extinguisher from the office",correct:!1}],explanation:"Covering the pan cuts off the air, and turning off the heat stops the oil from getting hotter. Water makes burning oil erupt in a fireball, moving the pan spreads the fire, and ABC powder is not made for cooking oil."},{question:"A fire under the hood is not going out. The kitchen has a hood suppression system and a wet chemical extinguisher. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Activate the hood system with the pull station, then the extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Use the extinguisher first and keep the hood system for a bigger fire",correct:!1},{text:"Switch off the extract fan and remove the filters to let the smoke out",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for the hood system to start by itself before doing anything",correct:!1}],explanation:"The hood system cuts off the gas or electricity and covers the cooking surfaces, the hood and the duct with extinguishing agent. The wet chemical extinguisher is designed for use after the system has been activated, as the sign next to it says."},{question:"Why must cooking appliances under the hood not be moved or swapped without the system installer?",options:[{text:"Heavier appliances can crack the floor tiles under the hood",correct:!1},{text:"The nozzles are aimed at specific appliances and may miss a new one",correct:!0},{text:"Moving them makes the kitchen noisier for the cooks and customers",correct:!1},{text:"New appliances always come with their own built-in fire system",correct:!1}],explanation:"The system protects the appliances it was designed for. If an appliance is moved or replaced, the installer must check that the nozzles still cover it."},{question:"Where should a restaurant's LPG cylinders and manifold be?",options:[{text:"In the basement, where they stay cool and out of the way of staff",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the kitchen under the range, close to the burners they feed",correct:!1},{text:"In the dining-room storeroom, behind the chairs that are stacked there",correct:!1},{text:"Outside in a ventilated place, upright and protected from heat",correct:!0}],explanation:"LPG is heavier than air and collects in low places such as basements. Cylinders are kept outside in a ventilated place, upright and away from heat, and a qualified technician makes the connections."},{question:"Why is cleaning grease from the hood, filters and duct a fire-safety task?",options:[{text:"Built-up grease can catch fire from cooking flames and spread in the duct",correct:!0},{text:"Grease only changes the smell of the food; it does not add to the fire risk",correct:!1},{text:"Clean ducts make the hood system go off by mistake far more often",correct:!1},{text:"A layer of grease protects the metal of the duct from the heat of cooking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flames from frying, grilling or flambéing can ignite grease in the hood and duct, and a fire in the duct can spread through the building. Regular cleaning removes this fuel."}]},{id:24,code:"BUS-124",icon:"Building",accent:"#6d28d9",title:"Fire safety in hotels and guest accommodation",subtitle:"Night alarms, guest information, staff roles and guests who need help",summary:"In a hotel, guests sleep in a building they do not know, and some of them cannot leave without help. That makes a fire at night the greatest danger. This lesson is for owners, managers, reception and night staff: what guests must be told, the room-door rule, what each member of staff does at night, how to help guests with disabilities, and how smoke alarms and fire doors protect everyone. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Why the night is the greatest danger",body:"A hotel fire plan must work at the worst possible time:",points:["Sleeping guests are slow to wake and may be confused, and they do not know the building or its exits.","Once the alarm sounds, there may be only a few minutes to get out safely.","Smoke spreads along corridors and up stairs; closed doors slow the spread of smoke, heat and fire.","At night the fewest staff are on duty, so the plan must work with the night team alone, and there must be enough staff to help guests who cannot leave by themselves."]},{heading:"2. Alarms, smoke detectors and fire doors",body:"These protect guests while they sleep, as long as they are kept working:",points:["Every guest room has smoke detection and an alarm loud enough to wake a sleeping guest.","Offer guests who are deaf or hard of hearing a room with a flashing-light or vibrating-pad alarm, where available.","Fire doors on corridors and stairs close by themselves. Never wedge or tie them open, and report at once a door that does not close fully.","A sprinkler system in each guest room gives extra protection where it is installed.","Check every day that escape routes are clear, exit doors open and the emergency lighting works; testing and service intervals are set by Civil Defense."]},{heading:"3. What every guest must know",body:"Give each guest the fire information at check-in:",points:["An escape plan on the inside of the room door shows where the room is and the routes to the nearest exits.","Tell guests what the fire alarm sounds like and where the two nearest exits are, and suggest that they count the doors from their room to the exit; this helps in the dark or in smoke.","Keep the room key by the bed and take it when you leave.","When the alarm sounds: leave at once, close the doors behind you and use the stairs, never the elevator.","Exit doors on escape routes open from the inside without a key at all times."]},{heading:"4. The room-door rule",body:"Teach it to staff and print it on the fire notice in every room:",points:["Before opening the room door, feel the door and the handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, or smoke is coming in around it, keep it closed.","If the door is cool, open it slowly and be ready to shut it at once if there is smoke or fire outside.","If the corridor is clear, leave, close the door behind you and go to the nearest exit; if there is smoke, keep low.","If every way out is blocked: stay in the room with the door closed, block the gaps around the door and the air vents with towels or bedding, switch off fans and air conditioning, call 115 and give the room number, and signal at the window.","Do not jump from an upper floor; wait for the firefighters."]},{heading:"5. Staff roles at night",body:"The night plan names who does what, even when only one or two staff are on duty:",points:["Reception confirms the alarm, calls 115 at once and gives the address, where the fire is and how many guests are in the hotel.","Reception takes the list of occupied rooms and the list of guests who need help to the assembly point.","Staff alert guests and guide them to the exits on their way out, and check rooms, toilets and corridors only where it is safe; they never enter smoke or open a hot door.","At the assembly point, compare the list of occupied rooms with the people present.","Meet the firefighters when they arrive and tell them which rooms may still be occupied, where guests who need help are waiting and about any special dangers, such as gas.","Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe."]},{heading:"6. Guests with disabilities",body:"Plan for them before the night they need it:",points:["At check-in, ask guests politely and privately whether they would need help to leave in an emergency, and keep a list of their names and room numbers at reception.","Where possible, give these guests a room on a low floor, close to an exit.","Never use an ordinary elevator in a fire. A guest who cannot use the stairs is moved away from the fire through the fire doors on the same floor, or waits with a member of staff in the refuge next to a protected stairway, as the hotel plan says.","Never leave a guest alone in a refuge, and tell the firefighters at once exactly where they are waiting.","The plan must not depend on the firefighters to rescue guests who are waiting: there must be enough trained staff to help them."]},{heading:"7. Training and drills",body:"At night, staff can only do what they have practiced:",points:["Train every new member of staff before their first night shift: the alarm, the exits, the fire doors, the room lists and their own role.","Hold drills, including at the times when the fewest staff are on duty, and practice as if one exit or stairway were blocked.","Keep the emergency plan and the room lists within easy reach at reception, and update the lists at every check-in and check-out.","How often drills are held and what training is required are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_hotel_night_evacuation.webp",title:"Night evacuation in a hotel",description:"A night staff member with a flashlight guides guests along the corridor to the exit stairs, with a closed fire door behind them"}],quiz:[{question:"What should the escape plan on the inside of a guest room door show?",options:[{text:"Where the room is and the routes to the two nearest exits",correct:!0},{text:"Only the hotel's telephone number and the checkout time",correct:!1},{text:"The way to the elevator and the way to the main entrance only",correct:!1},{text:"Where the hotel restaurant and the parking lot are located",correct:!1}],explanation:"The plan on the door shows where the room is and the routes to the exits, so that a guest can find the way quickly, even in the dark. Elevators are not used in a fire."},{question:"The alarm sounds at night, and a guest feels that the room door handle is hot. What should the guest do?",options:[{text:"Open the door quickly and run along the corridor to the nearest stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Open the door a little to look into the corridor, then decide what to do",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the door closed, seal the gaps, call 115 and signal for help",correct:!0},{text:"Go down in the elevator, because the corridor may be full of smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"A hot handle means fire or hot smoke behind the door. The closed door protects the guest: seal the gaps and vents, call 115, give the room number and signal at the window."},{question:"During a night evacuation, which task belongs to reception?",options:[{text:"Staying at the desk to answer guests' calls until the fire is out",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 115 and taking the list of occupied rooms to the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"Going into every room, including rooms full of smoke, to search them",correct:!1},{text:"Collecting the guests' luggage from their rooms before leaving the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reception calls 115 at once and takes the room lists, so that people can be counted at the assembly point and firefighters know which rooms may still be occupied. Nobody enters smoke."},{question:"A guest who uses a wheelchair is on the third floor when the alarm sounds. What is right?",options:[{text:"A staff member takes them down in the ordinary elevator, which is faster than the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Staff leave them alone in the refuge and wait outside for the firefighters to come",correct:!1},{text:"Staff tell them to stay in their room until the alarm stops ringing by itself",correct:!1},{text:"A staff member takes them to the refuge, stays with them and tells the firefighters",correct:!0}],explanation:"Ordinary elevators are not used in a fire. The guest is moved away from the fire or waits with a staff member in the refuge, is never left alone, and the firefighters know at once where they are."},{question:"Why must fire doors in hotel corridors never be wedged open?",options:[{text:"Open doors let cold air from the stairs into the guest corridors at night",correct:!1},{text:"A closed fire door slows smoke, heat and fire and protects the escape route",correct:!0},{text:"Open doors make guests hear the alarm far too loudly inside their rooms",correct:!1},{text:"Fire doors are heavy, and a wedge slowly damages their hinges over time",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire door protects only when it is closed: it slows the spread of smoke, heat and fire and keeps the escape route safe for guests. A door wedged open gives no such protection."}]},{id:25,code:"BUS-125",icon:"Fuel",accent:"#b45309",title:"Fuel station safety",subtitle:"Ignition control, static, spills, emergency stop and LPG",summary:"Fuel stations store and dispense large amounts of flammable liquid and gas. This module shows attendants, supervisors and managers how to control ignition sources and static electricity, deal with spills, use the emergency stop, fill LPG safely and act when a fire starts at the pump.",blocks:[{body:"Gasoline gives off flammable vapor even in cold weather (its flash point is about -43°C), and the vapor is heavier than air: it spreads along the ground and collects in low places such as drains, pits and basements. A spark, a flame, a cigarette or a running engine can ignite it. The rules for licensing, layout and equipment of fuel stations are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority: follow their requirements. This module covers what every attendant, supervisor and manager must do."},{heading:"1. Ignition control at the pumps",body:"Before and during fueling, the attendant makes sure that:",points:["The engine of the vehicle is switched off at the pump.","No one smokes or uses a lighter or open flame near the pumps, and no-smoking and engine-off signs are posted close to the pumps.","Control is constant: you can see every vehicle and every person at the pumps, and you start a pump only when there is no danger.","You do not fuel when the engine is running, when someone is smoking nearby or when the container is not approved; call the supervisor if the customer refuses.","Phones: apply the station's own rule (some guidance asks that phones and radios are not used at the pumps), and keep your attention on the main ignition sources: smoking, flames, running engines and static electricity.","The forecourt is kept clean, with no rags, cardboard or other flammable materials near the pumps."]},{heading:"2. Static electricity and portable containers",body:"A static spark at the pump nozzle can ignite fuel vapor:",points:["Customers should stay outside the vehicle while fueling: sliding across the seat builds up static. If they must get back in, they touch a metal part of the car away from the filling point before they touch the nozzle again.","Fill a portable container only when it stands on the ground, never in a car trunk, on a seat or in the back of a pickup truck.","Touch the container with the nozzle before opening it, and keep the nozzle in contact with the container opening while filling.","Fill only suitable, approved fuel containers, never bottles, buckets or food containers; which containers and sizes are allowed is set by the licensing authority.","Close the container tightly; fuel containers and gas cylinders are never carried in the passenger compartment of a car."]},{heading:"3. Fuel spills",body:"Trained staff can deal with a small spill, for example from overfilling; a large spill is an emergency:",points:["1. Stop the flow: release the nozzle or stop the pump; if it does not stop, press the emergency stop.","2. Keep people and vehicles away; no one nearby starts an engine, smokes or switches on any electrical equipment.","3. Wearing gloves, cover the spill with sand or absorbent material from the spill kit; do not walk through the fuel.","4. Do not wash the fuel away with water: water carries it into drains, where the vapor can collect and explode.","5. Collect the soaked material in a closed metal container: it is still flammable.","Call 115 at once if the fuel reaches a drain or the street, if fuel vapor is smelled inside a building, if the spill is large or if it catches fire."]},{heading:"4. The emergency stop",body:"The emergency stop cuts the power to all pumps at once. Where it must be installed and how often it is tested are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority:",points:["Every member of staff knows where each emergency stop is, inside at the cash desk and outside on the forecourt, and has practiced pressing it.","Press it for any spill you cannot stop, any fire, any leak at the LPG point and any vehicle that hits a pump.","Keep it visible, clearly labeled and never blocked by goods, displays or parked cars.","The emergency plan of the station includes a site map showing the tanks, pumps, emergency stops, extinguishers, spill kit and assembly point.","Every attendant knows the exact address of the station and how to describe its location to 115."]},{heading:"5. LPG (autogas) filling",body:"LPG is stored as a liquid under pressure. A leak forms a gas cloud that is heavier than air and can ignite far from the leak. In the project's reference profile, LPG fires are NFPA Class B (gases); in the EN 2 system they are Class C.",points:["Only trained staff fill LPG; customers do not fill it themselves unless the licensing authority allows it.","The attendant stays with the vehicle for the whole filling, until the equipment is switched off and the valves are closed.","Engines are off, and no one smokes or uses a flame near the filling point.","Wear gloves and eye protection: liquid LPG causes cold burns on the skin.","Check the vehicle's filling valve before connecting; if a connector leaks, stop filling.","If gas keeps leaking or catches fire: press the emergency stop, move people away and call 115. Close the gas supply only if it can be reached safely; never put out a gas flame whose supply cannot be stopped."]},{heading:"6. Fire at the pump",body:"If a vehicle or a pump catches fire:",points:["Press the emergency stop at once and raise the fire alarm.","Tell the driver to leave the nozzle in the car and get away; get everyone out of the vehicles and away from the pumps.","Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) and give the station's name and address first.","Keep everyone at least 30 meters (100 feet) from the burning vehicle, and keep other vehicles off the forecourt.","Do not open the hood or trunk of a burning car: fresh air makes the fire grow.","A trained attendant may use an extinguisher only on a small fire that has just started, with an extinguisher whose label covers burning fuel (Class B; NFPA B liquids = EN B), with the way out behind them and the alarm already raised. Start from the distance on the label; if the fire does not go out at once, get away.","Practice these steps in regular drills with all staff, and keep a record of each drill."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_fuel_station_safety.webp",title:"Safe fueling at the pump",description:"Engine off, container on the ground, emergency stop and extinguisher ready"}],quiz:[{question:"A customer got back into the car during fueling and is now returning to the pump. What should the customer do before touching the nozzle?",options:[{text:"Touch bare metal on the car, away from the filler",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe the nozzle handle with a dry cloth first",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the nozzle out quickly and restart the pump",correct:!1},{text:"Wait inside the car until the pump clicks off by itself",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sliding across the seat builds up static electricity. Touching metal away from the filling point discharges it before the hand reaches the nozzle, where a spark could ignite the vapor."},{question:"Fuel is pouring onto the forecourt from a nozzle that will not shut off. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Wash the fuel away toward the drain with a water hose",correct:!1},{text:"Stop the flow at the pump or with the emergency stop",correct:!0},{text:"Start the car and drive it away from the spill",correct:!1},{text:"Spread absorbent around it but leave the pump running",correct:!1}],explanation:"First stop the source, then keep ignition sources and people away and absorb the spill. Water carries fuel into drains, where vapor collects, and starting an engine can ignite the vapor."},{question:"Where must a portable fuel container be while it is filled?",options:[{text:"In the back of a pickup truck, ready to drive away",correct:!1},{text:"On the back seat, held steady by a passenger",correct:!1},{text:"On the ground, with the nozzle touching it",correct:!0},{text:"In the car trunk, with the trunk lid fully open",correct:!1}],explanation:"Inside a vehicle, carpets or plastic bed liners insulate the container, so the static from the flowing fuel cannot escape and can spark at the nozzle (NIOSH). On the ground, with the nozzle touching it, the charge goes safely to ground."},{question:"During LPG filling, gas starts to leak from the connector. What should the attendant do?",options:[{text:"Stop filling, close the valves, keep people and engines away",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the filling quickly and tighten the connector afterward",correct:!1},{text:"Spray the leak with a CO₂ extinguisher to cool the connector",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the driver to start the engine and drive off at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Leaking LPG forms a gas cloud that can ignite. Stop the filling, close the gas supply if it can be reached safely and keep ignition sources away; if the leak does not stop, press the emergency stop and call 115."},{question:"A car catches fire at a pump. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Pull the nozzle out of the car before doing anything else",correct:!1},{text:"Open the hood so the fire can be seen and reached",correct:!1},{text:"Spray water from a hose onto the burning fuel",correct:!1},{text:"Press the emergency stop, clear the area and call 115",correct:!0}],explanation:"The emergency stop cuts the fuel to all pumps. Leave the nozzle in place, keep the hood closed and keep everyone well away; water spreads burning fuel."}]},{id:26,code:"IND-126",icon:"Package",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Warehouse and storage fire safety",subtitle:"Stacking, clearances, sprinklers, battery charging and security",summary:"In a warehouse a fire can climb a stack of goods to the roof in minutes. This module shows staff, supervisors and managers how to stack and store goods safely, keep sprinklers working, charge forklift batteries, handle lithium-ion batteries and hazardous goods, and protect the building at night.",blocks:[{body:"In stacked goods and high racking, flames spread quickly upward to the top of the stack and then sideways to every level; in tests with boxes on 10 m high racking, fires often reached the top within two minutes (HM Government fire safety guide for factories and warehouses). The fire load of a warehouse depends on what is stored and is often higher than in homes or offices. The rules below are general good practice; every building must first meet the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."},{heading:"1. Stacking and clearances",body:"Stored goods must be stable and must not block people, sprinklers or firefighters:",points:["Stack goods so they are stable: blocked, interlocked and limited in height so they cannot slide or collapse.","Do not overstock: every storage location has a written maximum.","Leave at least 1 meter of clear space between the top of the stock and the roof, roof beams or light fittings (UK HSE guidance).","Under sprinkler heads, keep the clearance set by the sprinkler design; OSHA's general minimum is 45 centimeters (18 inches), and some sprinkler types need more.","Racking and stock must never shield the sprinkler heads.","Do not pile combustible goods against electrical equipment, light fittings or heaters, even when they are switched off.","Keep empty pallets, packaging and waste in a designated area away from the building, exits and windows, or in a sprinklered area."]},{heading:"2. Aisles, exits and fire doors",body:"Clear routes let people get out and let the 115 teams reach the fire:",points:["Keep aisles and passageways clear and in good repair; permanent aisles are marked on the floor.","Never store goods in escape routes or in front of exits, fire doors, fire shutters, extinguishers or alarm call points.","Leave a clear gap between stored goods and the inside walls so firefighters can reach a fire (UK guidance: 0.5 m).","Fire exits must open from the inside without a key whenever anyone is in the building.","Only trained and authorized operators drive forklifts, and pedestrians use the marked walkways."]},{heading:"3. Sprinklers: keep them working",body:"Sprinklers control a fire automatically, even at night when no one is there, but only if nothing stops them:",points:["Never hang anything from sprinkler heads or pipes, and never paint, cover or knock them; report a damaged or leaking head at once.","Sprinkler control valves stay fully open. Only an authorized person may close one, under the site's procedure for shutting down fire protection, with extra fire watch until it is reopened.","If you find a valve closed or a head blocked, tell the supervisor at once: the area may be without protection.","Sprinklers open only where the heat of the fire reaches them, so a fire under a shielded head can grow unseen.","Inspection, testing and maintenance follow NFPA 25 and the requirements of Civil Defense; keep the records.","Some products must not be stored in sprinklered areas at all; the safety data sheet (SDS) says so."]},{heading:"4. Forklift battery charging",body:"Charging lead-acid batteries gives off hydrogen, a highly flammable gas; if it collects and meets a spark or flame, it can explode:",points:["Charge only in the designated charging area: well ventilated (with ventilation high above the batteries), clear of stored goods and ignition sources.","No smoking, no flames, no hot work and no phones in the charging area.","Switch the charger off before connecting or disconnecting the battery, to avoid sparks.","Keep tools and other metal objects off the top of batteries, and take off watches, rings and other metal items before working on them.","Open the battery cover while charging, and wear the protective equipment set for the task (gloves, apron and eye protection against acid).","Change the LPG cylinders of gas-powered forklifts outside, away from ignition sources.","Only trained staff change or charge batteries."]},{heading:"5. Lithium-ion batteries",body:"Damaged, overheated or wrongly charged lithium-ion batteries can catch fire or explode, and heat from one failing cell can spread to the next (thermal runaway):",points:["Follow the manufacturer's instructions for storage, use and charging, and use only the charger made for that battery.","Store batteries and devices in dry, cool places away from heaters and other heat sources, and take them off charge when they are full.","Look for warning signs: bulging or cracking, hissing, leaking, rising temperature or smoke. A battery with any of these signs is taken out of use at once.","If it is safe to handle, move it to the designated place away from flammable materials, for example a fire-resistant metal container with sand, and send it for disposal by the approved route.","If a battery is smoking or burning, do not pick it up: warn others, raise the alarm, get out and call 115. Lithium-ion cells contain no lithium metal and are cooled with plenty of water; a fire in stored batteries is for the 115 teams.","Battery fires can flare up again after they seem to be out; tell the 115 teams where batteries are stored."],critical:!0},{heading:"6. Housekeeping and hazardous goods",body:"Less waste means less fuel for a fire, and correct storage keeps a small incident small:",points:["Keep storage areas free of loose packaging and rubbish; put waste in suitable containers and remove it regularly.","No smoking in storage areas.","Read the label and the safety data sheet (SDS) of every hazardous product before storing it; section 7 of the SDS gives the storage conditions and incompatible materials.","Keep incompatible substances apart: stored together, they can make a fire grow faster or release toxic smoke.","Do not put damaged or leaking containers into storage; place them in a tray or bund and follow the supplier's instructions.","Keep an up-to-date record of which hazardous goods are stored where, kept where it can be handed to the 115 teams in an emergency."]},{heading:"7. Night security and the alarm",body:"A fire that starts when the building is closed can grow unseen, and empty buildings are a target for arson:",points:["Keep the outside well lit and the perimeter secure; secure doors, windows and roof openings without blocking the escape routes.","Do not place large waste containers against the building; keep waste bins in a locked compound away from it.","Lock away flammable liquids and gases so intruders cannot use them.","The fire alarm and the intruder alarm are monitored, and someone acts on every alarm at once.","Guards know how to raise the alarm, how to call 115 with the exact address and how to meet the 115 teams at the gate with the keys and the site plan.","Report people acting suspiciously and any small unexplained fires; they can be a warning of arson.","Review the fire risk assessment at least once a year and after every major change in what you store or how you store it."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_warehouse_fire_safety.webp",title:"An orderly, fire-safe warehouse",description:"Clear aisles, clear space under the sprinklers and a separate charging area"}],quiz:[{question:"Under UK HSE guidance, how much clear space should be left between the top of stored goods and the roof, beams or light fittings?",options:[{text:"At least 1 meter",correct:!0},{text:"At least 10 centimeters",correct:!1},{text:"At least 25 centimeters",correct:!1},{text:"None, if the stack is stable",correct:!1}],explanation:"HSE warehousing guidance asks for at least 1 m between stock tops and the roof, roof beams or light fittings; below sprinklers, the clearance set by the sprinkler design applies (OSHA's general minimum is 45 cm)."},{question:"Which practice is correct when charging forklift batteries?",options:[{text:"Charge between the racks so the truck is ready for the next shift",correct:!1},{text:"Disconnect the battery while the charger is still switched on",correct:!1},{text:"Use the ventilated charging area, with no smoking or flames",correct:!0},{text:"Rest metal tools on the battery top so they are close at hand",correct:!1}],explanation:"Charging gives off hydrogen. A designated, ventilated area without ignition sources keeps it from collecting and igniting; switching the charger off before disconnecting and keeping metal tools off the battery prevent sparks."},{question:"During a stock check you find a sprinkler control valve closed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Report it to the supervisor at once so it is reopened safely",correct:!0},{text:"Leave it closed, since the fire alarm will warn of any fire",correct:!1},{text:"Open it halfway so the water pressure in the pipes stays low",correct:!1},{text:"Hang a note on it and check it again at next month's inspection",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed control valve can leave a whole area without sprinkler protection. It must be reported at once and reopened by an authorized person under the site's procedure."},{question:"A lithium-ion battery in storage is swollen and getting hot. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Take it out of use; if it smokes, raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Put it back on charge so that the cells can balance and cool down",correct:!1},{text:"Wrap it in cardboard and leave it on the shelf for collection",correct:!1},{text:"Spray it once with CO₂ and return it to the shelf",correct:!1}],explanation:"Swelling and heat are warning signs of battery failure. Take the battery out of use and, if it is safe to handle, isolate it away from flammable materials; if it smokes or burns, do not touch it: raise the alarm, get out and call 115."},{question:"Which measure best protects a closed warehouse against arson?",options:[{text:"Large waste containers placed against the wall, where guards can see them",correct:!1},{text:"The alarm switched off at night to avoid false alarms",correct:!1},{text:"Waste kept away from the building and a monitored alarm",correct:!0},{text:"Flammable liquids left near the door, ready for the morning delivery",correct:!1}],explanation:"Arsonists often use waste and flammable liquids found on site. Keeping waste containers away from the building, locking away flammables and having alarms that are monitored and acted on reduce the risk (HM Government guide)."}]},{id:27,code:"IND-127",icon:"HardHat",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Construction site safety",subtitle:"Fire plan, hot work, temporary electrics, cylinders, excavations and UXO",summary:"Buildings under construction often have no working sprinklers or alarms yet, but they are full of ignition sources and combustible materials. This module shows workers, supervisors and managers how to plan for fire; control hot work, temporary electrics, fuel and gas cylinders; recognize danger at scaffolds and excavations; give first aid; and act when a suspicious object is found while digging.",blocks:[{body:"According to NFPA, the leading causes of fires in buildings under construction are cooking equipment, electrical distribution and lighting equipment, and heating equipment. Sprinklers, smoke detection and fire alarms may not be working yet, and open sites attract trespassers and arson. Site rules, permits and licenses are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority: follow their requirements. This module gives the basic rules for everyone on site."},{heading:"1. Site fire plan and assembly point",body:"Before work starts, the main contractor in control of the site prepares the fire plan:",points:["A fire risk assessment and a written emergency plan, ready before work starts and updated as the building grows.","One named person manages fire prevention on site (in NFPA 241: the fire prevention program manager).","Escape routes from every work area, kept clear and signed and never locked while people are on site; wherever practicable, two routes in different directions.","A fire alarm that everyone can hear above the site noise, and fire instruction notices at fire points, site entrances and canteens.","Fire points with suitable extinguishers, and nominated people trained to use them.","An assembly point where everyone is counted, by a head count or a sweep of the area.","Fire drills to test the plan and the training; every new worker and visitor learns the plan at induction."]},{heading:"2. Hot work: permit and fire watch",body:"Welding, cutting, grinding and torch work start many site fires. Use another method if it can do the job; if not:",points:["Work only with a hot work permit issued by the person authorized to approve it.","Clear combustible materials within 11 meters (35 feet) of the work, protect them with fire-resistant blankets or curtains, or move the work to a safe area.","Keep a suitable fire extinguisher ready at the work point.","A fire watch stays during the work and during breaks, and for at least 1 hour after the work ends; the permit authorizing individual may order a longer watch (NFPA 51B).","Plan hot work to finish well before the end of the working day, so the checks can be done before the site closes (UK HSE: 2 hours before)."]},{heading:"3. Temporary electrics",body:"Temporary site wiring causes fires and electric shocks when it is overloaded, damaged or altered by unqualified people:",points:["Temporary installations are designed, installed, changed and checked only by a qualified electrician; no one else adds to them or alters them.","Sockets used by workers are protected by a residual current device (RCD), which cuts the power quickly when current leaks to ground; press its test button regularly. An RCD reduces the risk but does not replace a safe installation.","Do not overload sockets or multi-way adapters, do not plug extension leads into each other, and unwind cable reels fully before use.","Use suitable flexible cable for extension leads, supported off the ground; take damaged leads and tools out of use instead of taping them.","Do not run temporary cables or distribution boards through escape routes, and keep rubbish away from distribution boards."]},{heading:"4. Fuel and gas cylinders on site",body:"Fuel, LPG, acetylene and oxygen bring a lot of stored energy onto the site:",points:["Keep only the minimum quantity on site, in a secure, well-ventilated store away from combustible materials and work areas, never inside the building under construction.","Use only approved containers for fuel, and never store fuel in exits, stairways or passageways.","Refuel machines in a designated place, with engines off, no smoking and a fire extinguisher nearby.","Keep gas cylinders upright and secured against falling, with the valve caps on when they are not in use, and away from sparks, hot slag and flames.","Store oxygen cylinders apart from fuel-gas cylinders: at least 6 meters (20 feet) apart, or behind a fire-resistant barrier (OSHA).","Lock fuel and cylinders away at the end of the day so intruders cannot use them.","If a cylinder leaks or burns: close the valve only if it can be reached safely; never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped; get everyone away and call 115."]},{heading:"5. Scaffolds and excavations: recognize danger and stop",body:"Falls and trench collapses kill construction workers. Designing scaffolds and trench supports is work for competent specialists; everyone on site must recognize danger and stop:",points:["Use a scaffold only after a competent person has inspected it before the shift; never use one that is damaged, incomplete or missing guardrails, and never alter it yourself.","Work at 1.8 meters (6 feet) or more above a lower level, or near an open edge or hole, needs fall protection: guardrails, covers or a harness attached to a secure anchor.","Never enter an unprotected trench. Under OSHA, excavations 1.5 meters (5 feet) deep or more need a protective system designed for the site, unless they are cut in stable rock.","Keep soil heaps, materials and machines at least 0.6 meters (2 feet) back from the edge.","A competent person inspects excavations every day and after rain.","At signs of collapse (cracks in the ground, bulging walls, water seeping in, falling soil): get out at once, keep others out and tell the supervisor; work restarts only when the danger is controlled."]},{heading:"6. Access for 115 and first aid",body:"In a fire or an injury, every minute counts:",points:["Keep the site entrance, gates and access roads clear for 115 fire engines and ambulances; do not park or store materials on them.","Keep fire hydrants, water points and firefighting equipment visible and easy to reach.","Large sites contact Civil Defense before work starts and whenever access or hazards change, and keep an information pack (site plan, hazards, stored fuel, gas and batteries) ready for the 115 teams.","A person trained in first aid and a stocked first aid kit are available on site.","Post the site address, directions and the numbers 115 and 122 by the phones, in the site office and at the entrance.","After a fall from height, do not move the injured person unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed; call 122."]},{heading:"7. Suspicious object found while digging",body:"Old explosive ordnance can be found while digging, even in places thought to be safe, and it can explode if it is moved or hit. If a machine, a worker or a tool uncovers a suspicious object:",points:["Stop the machine and the work at once.","Never touch, move, lift, dig around or pull out the object, and never put it in a bucket or a vehicle.","Clear everyone from the area and keep people and machines out.","Move well away (at least 50 meters, UNMAS) before using a phone or radio, then call 115 and follow the instructions of Civil Defense.","From a safe place, note where the object is and tell the 115 teams; work restarts only when the authorities say the site is safe."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_construction_site_fire_safety.webp",title:"A fire-ready construction site",description:"Hot work permit with a fire watch, secured cylinders and a clear access road"}],quiz:[{question:"Under NFPA 51B, how long must the fire watch stay after hot work ends?",options:[{text:"At least 1 hour, or longer if the permit requires it",correct:!0},{text:"Until the welder has packed up the tools and left the site",correct:!1},{text:"About 10 minutes, if no smoke can be seen by then",correct:!1},{text:"No watch is needed when an extinguisher is close by",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sparks can smolder in hidden places and start a fire later. NFPA 51B requires the fire watch for at least 1 hour after the work, and the permit authorizing individual can order a longer watch."},{question:"An extension lead on site has damaged insulation. What should be done?",options:[{text:"Take it out of use and have it replaced by a qualified person",correct:!0},{text:"Wrap the damaged part with insulating tape and carry on working",correct:!1},{text:"Plug it into a second lead so each lead carries less current",correct:!1},{text:"Use it only for small hand tools until the job is finished",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged leads cause shocks and fires. They are taken out of use and replaced or properly repaired by a competent person, never taped (UK HSE); an RCD reduces the risk but does not make a damaged lead safe."},{question:"Cracks appear in the ground and soil starts falling into a trench where a colleague is working. What is correct?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, keep others out and tell the supervisor",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the section quickly before the trench wall moves",correct:!1},{text:"Climb in and hold the wall back with spare timber boards",correct:!1},{text:"Park the excavator at the edge so its weight holds the soil",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cracks and falling soil are signs of collapse. Workers leave at once and stay out until a competent person has made the trench safe; a heavy machine at the edge makes a collapse more likely."},{question:"An excavator uncovers a rusty object that looks like a shell. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Lift it gently with the bucket and set it to one side",correct:!1},{text:"Brush the soil off it to check whether it is real",correct:!1},{text:"Stop work, clear the area and call 115 from far away",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it to the site office until the police arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"Explosive ordnance can explode when it is moved or hit. Stop at once, clear everyone away, never move it and call 115 from well away, avoiding phone or radio use close to the object (UNMAS)."},{question:"Which arrangement is correct for gas cylinders on a construction site?",options:[{text:"Laid on their side so they cannot fall over",correct:!1},{text:"In the stairway, because it is the coolest place on site",correct:!1},{text:"Oxygen and acetylene chained together in a single store",correct:!1},{text:"Upright and secured, caps on, away from sparks and heat",correct:!0}],explanation:"Cylinders are kept upright and secured with valve caps on, away from sparks and heat, and oxygen is kept apart from fuel gas (OSHA 1926.350). Stairways are escape routes and must stay free of fuel and cylinders."}]},{id:28,code:"BUS-128",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#b91c1c",title:"Fire blankets, wheeled and vehicle extinguishers",subtitle:"When and how to use them safely, and why lithium-ion battery fires need special care",summary:"Besides hand-held extinguishers, workplaces use fire blankets, large wheeled extinguishers and extinguishers in vehicles. Each has its limits. This lesson explains when and how to use them safely, and what to do when a lithium-ion battery overheats or catches fire.",blocks:[{body:`Every piece of fire equipment has a job and limits. The same go/no-go check applies to all of them: the fire is small and just starting, the equipment is right for it, the exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, and you are trained. If any answer is no, get out. Which extinguisher standard is enforced in Duhok (NFPA 10 or EN 3) and who may service extinguishers are not yet confirmed; follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.`},{heading:"1. Fire blanket: a small pan fire",body:"A fire blanket is a sheet of fire-resistant fabric in a quick-release pack. It smothers a small fire in a pan or pot by cutting off the air:",points:["Keep it near the kitchen door, not next to the stove, so that you can reach it and still get out.","Pull the tabs down sharply to release the blanket.","Hold it in front of you with the edges rolled over your hands, to protect your hands and face.","Place it gently over the pan; do not throw it. Make sure it covers the whole pan.","Turn off the heat only if you can reach the control safely.","Leave the blanket in place and do not move the pan. Do not lift the blanket to look until everything has cooled completely.","Call 115 even if the fire seems to be out; if it does not go out at once, get out and close the door. Never put water on burning oil."]},{heading:"2. Fire blanket: clothes on fire",body:"If a person's clothes catch fire:",points:["The person stops, drops to the ground, covers their face with their hands and rolls until the flames are out.","If you have a fire blanket, wrap it around the flames and the person, and help them drop to the ground and roll.","Protect your own hands with the edges of the blanket.","Then cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes, do not burst blisters and call 122.","A fire blanket is used once and then replaced. Buy one whose label shows the standard it meets, for example EN 1869; a blanket with no standard mark may not work. Which standard is accepted locally is not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"3. Wheeled extinguishers",body:"Wheeled extinguishers carry far more agent than a hand-held unit, on a cart with a long hose. They protect places such as fuel stores, repair workshops and loading areas:",points:["Only staff trained on that model use it: the controls, the order of the steps and the range differ between models, so follow the label.","Work in pairs where you can: one person controls the cart and the valve as the label shows, the other handles the hose and nozzle. Some models are made for one trained person.","Take the unit to a position within the range shown on the label, with your exit behind you, and follow the label's steps in order.","The go/no-go check is the same: a wheeled unit does not make a large fire safe to fight.","After any use, it is recharged by a qualified technician."]},{heading:"4. Vehicle extinguishers",body:"Trucks, forklifts, buses and company cars often carry a small extinguisher, but most vehicle fires are not for the driver to fight:",points:["Pull over as soon as it is safe, switch off the engine, get everyone out and move well away from the vehicle and the traffic, at least 30 meters (100 feet).","Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) and warn other traffic; never go back for belongings.","Never open the hood or the trunk if you think the fire is inside: fresh air makes it flare up.","Use the vehicle extinguisher only on a small fire that has just started, only if you are trained, everyone is out and your way back is clear. For a fire in the engine compartment, keep the hood closed; some fire services advise aiming through the grille or the gap at the edge of the hood.","If in doubt, do not tackle the fire. Never put water on an engine fire.","Forklifts and other battery-powered vehicles: if the battery is involved, treat it as a battery fire (section 5)."]},{heading:"5. Lithium-ion battery fires",body:"Lithium-ion batteries are in phones, laptops, power tools, e-bikes, forklifts and battery storage systems. When damaged, overcharged or faulty, they can overheat, give off toxic gas, catch fire or explode:",points:["Warning signs: a strange smell, heat, swelling or a change in shape or color, popping noises or smoke. Stop using or charging it at once.","If it is safe, unplug it and move it away from anything that can burn.","If it is burning: get everyone out, close the door and call 115 from outside; say that it is a battery fire.","Do not breathe the smoke: a damaged battery can give off toxic gas that you may not see.","Cooling with large amounts of water is the recommended method, but only trained people with the right equipment do it; ordinary ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop the fire.","Lithium-ion batteries are not Class D: Class D agents are only for lithium-metal batteries and burning metals.","Prevention: use only the maker's charger or one the maker approves, charge on a hard surface away from things that burn and away from exits, and take damaged batteries out of use."],critical:!0},{heading:"6. Checks and service",body:"Keep all this equipment ready:",points:["In the regular visual check, look at fire blankets too: in place, easy to reach, pack undamaged and tabs hanging free.","Wheeled and vehicle extinguishers get the same visual check as other extinguishers: in place, pin and seal intact, gauge in the green if there is one, no damage.","Recharge after any use; yearly maintenance and pressure tests are done by a qualified technician. The local service rules and who may service extinguishers are not yet confirmed.","Replace a used fire blanket and report any used extinguisher at once."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_fire_blanket_pan.webp",title:"Using a fire blanket on a small pan fire",description:"A worker in a staff kitchen holds a fire blanket in front of her, hands protected behind the fabric, and lays it gently over a burning pan; the door behind her is open."}],quiz:[{question:"A small pan of oil catches fire on the stove. You have a fire blanket. What is the right method?",options:[{text:"Protect your hands with the edges, lay it gently over the pan and leave it there",correct:!0},{text:"Throw the blanket onto the pan from across the kitchen to keep your distance",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the pan, then lift the blanket after a minute to check the fire is out",correct:!1},{text:"Pour a glass of water on the pan first, then cover it with the blanket",correct:!1}],explanation:"Place the blanket gently over the pan with your hands protected behind it, turn off the heat if you can do so safely, leave it until everything has cooled and call 115. Never water on burning oil."},{question:"A coworker's clothes catch fire. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Get them to stop, drop and roll, wrapping a fire blanket around the flames if there is one",correct:!0},{text:"Tell them to run to the nearest tap so that the running water can put the flames out",correct:!1},{text:"Spray them with a CO₂ extinguisher aimed straight at their face and chest until it stops",correct:!1},{text:"Pull off their burning clothes with your bare hands as quickly as you possibly can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stop, drop and roll smothers the flames, and a fire blanket wrapped around them helps; protect your own hands with its edges. Then cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{question:"Who should use a wheeled extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Staff trained on that model, following its label, ideally working in pairs",correct:!0},{text:"Any employee who happens to be nearest to the fire when the alarm sounds",correct:!1},{text:"Only the security guard, because wheeled units are kept near the main gate",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone who has used a small hand-held extinguisher at home at least once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Wheeled units differ from model to model. Only staff trained on that model use them, following the label, ideally in pairs, and only after the same go/no-go check."},{question:"Smoke is coming from under the hood of a company truck. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Stop, switch off, get everyone out and away, call 115 and keep the hood closed",correct:!0},{text:"Open the hood fully so that you can aim the extinguisher straight at the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Keep driving to the nearest workshop so that a mechanic can look at it",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water from a bottle onto the engine through the gap under the hood",correct:!1}],explanation:"Opening the hood lets in fresh air and the fire flares up. Get everyone out and at least 30 meters (100 feet) away, call 115, and never put water on an engine fire."},{question:"A power-tool battery on a charging shelf is swelling and giving off smoke. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Unplug and move it if that is safe; if it catches fire, get out and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Cover it with Class D powder, because lithium-ion batteries are metal fires",correct:!1},{text:"Put it in a metal cupboard and shut the door tight so that the smoke stays inside",correct:!1},{text:"Keep charging it, because the swelling goes down once it is fully charged",correct:!1}],explanation:"Swelling and smoke are warning signs. If it is safe, unplug it and move it away from anything that can burn; if it catches fire, get everyone out, close the door and call 115. Lithium-ion batteries are not Class D."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
